        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Here\n$\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ is the Schwartz space of smooth complex functions\nwhose coordinate-weighted derivatives are all bounded, as defined in\n[[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]].\nFix an oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski space and an inertial coordinate\nchart as in [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]. Use coordinates\n$x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$ and metric $\\eta=\\mathrm{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, matching the\n$(-+++)$ convention of the SR suppliers. Some QFT sources use its negative\n$(+---)$; the Lorentz group is unchanged, and the phase is fixed explicitly\nbelow.\nCoordinates $x$ have length units, momenta have inverse-length units, and the\nFourier phase below is dimensionless. A parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ has inverse-length\nunits and is only a mathematical shell parameter; it is not identified with a\nphysical rest mass. For $\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3$, set\n$$\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2},\\qquad p_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k).$$\nThe future mass shell is\n$$H_\\mu^+=\\{(p^0,\\mathbf p): (p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2=\\mu^2,\\ p^0>0\\}.$$\nWhen $\\mu=0$, its apex is omitted; thus $\\mathbf k\\ne0$ in the massless\nparameterization. The scalar shell pairing is\n$$p\\circ x:=p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x=-\\eta(p,x).$$\nDefine the measure and one-particle Hilbert space by\n$$d\\nu_\\mu(p_\\mu(\\mathbf k))= \\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)},\\qquad H_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu;\\mathbb C),$$\nwhere the omitted massless apex has measure zero. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb\nR^4)$ define its pointwise shell Fourier restriction by\n$$K_\\mu f(p)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}f(x)e^{ip\\circ x}\\,d^4x.$$\nThe Fourier convention is exactly the displayed integral, with no prefactor.\nIf $\\widehat f(\\xi^0,\\boldsymbol\\xi)=\n\\int f(x)e^{-2\\pi i(x^0\\xi^0+\\mathbf x\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\xi)}\\,d^4x$ denotes\nthe standard transform in\n[[cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space]],\nthen\n$$\\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p):=\\int f(x)e^{i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)}\\,d^4x =\\widehat f\\!\\left(-\\frac{p^0}{2\\pi},\\frac{\\mathbf p}{2\\pi}\\right).$$\nIts inverse is\n$$f(x)=(2\\pi)^{-4}\\int_{\\mathbb R^4} \\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p)e^{-i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)} \\,dp^0\\,d^3\\mathbf p.$$\nThus this convention is a time reflection and rescaling of the cited\nSchwartz automorphism and has the stated reciprocal normalization.\nThe proper orthochronous Lorentz group consists of linear maps preserving\n$\\eta$, orientation and the chosen future cone. A Poincare map is\n$x\\mapsto a+\\Lambda x$ with $a\\in\\mathbb R^4$ and $\\Lambda$ in that group.\n\nThis is a mathematical free scalar one-particle construction. It adopts the\nchosen coordinates, measure and Fourier convention; it asserts no experimental\nobservation, physical mass identification or measurement outcome.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "An **oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space** is a quadruple\n$(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ with the following data. In this notation $V$ includes its\nspecified orientation; forgetting that orientation gives only the underlying\nreal vector space, not the full datum $V$ of the model.\n\n1. A nonempty affine space $A$ over a $4$-dimensional real vector space $V$,\n   with action $(p,v)\\mapsto p+v$ and displacement $q-p\\in V$\n   ([[def-affine-space-and-displacement]]). In particular $V$ is a real vector\n   space of dimension $4$.\n2. A Lorentz form $\\eta$ on $V$, i.e. a nondegenerate symmetric bilinear form of\n   inertia $(3,1,0)$ ([[def-lorentz-form-and-inertia]]). Its associated quadratic\n   form is $q(v)=\\eta(v,v)$.\n3. An **orientation** of $V$: a positive ray of the determinant line\n   $\\Lambda^4V$, fixed once and for all\n   ([[def-determinant-line-orientation-of-a-finite-dimensional-real-vector-space]]).\n4. A **time orientation** $C^+$ of $(V,\\eta)$: one of the two connected\n   components of the timelike set $T=\\{v\\in V:\\eta(v,v)<0\\}$, the other being\n   $C^-:=T\\setminus C^+$ ([[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]]).\n\nThe following terminology is attached to such a quadruple.\n\n- The elements $p$ of $A$ are the **events**; for events $p,q$ the vector\n  $q-p\\in V$ is the **displacement from $p$ to $q$**.\n- The **interval** of $p,q\\in A$ is the real number\n  $I(p,q):=\\eta(q-p,q-p)=q(q-p)$.\n- Events $p,q$ are **timelike**, **null**, **spacelike** or **causally\n  related** when the displacement $q-p$ is timelike, null, spacelike or causal\n  in the sense of [[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]]; they are\n  **future-related** when $q-p$ is future causal and **past-related** when\n  $q-p$ is past causal in the sense of\n  [[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]].\n\n**No point of $A$ is distinguished.** For every $v\\in V$ the translation\n$\\tau_v:A\\to A$, $\\tau_v(p):=p+v$, is a bijection, since it is inverse to\n$\\tau_{-v}$: $\\tau_{-v}(\\tau_v(p))=(p+v)+(-v)=p+(v-v)=p$ and symmetrically, by\nthe affine action axioms ([[def-affine-space-and-displacement]]). Displacements\nare translation invariant, $\\tau_v(q)-\\tau_v(p)=q-p$, so every $\\tau_v$ preserves\nthe interval, the causal classification of every ordered pair, and the sets\n$C^+$, $C^-$ (which are subsets of $V$, independent of the point of $A$); it\nchanges only the labelling of points. The mathematical structure of the model is\nexactly the quadruple $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$; no derived physical quantity (proper\ntime, four-velocity, momentum, energy, charge or any field) is part of this\ndefinition, and all later structure of the framework (frames, charts, tensor\nfields, worldlines) is built on this model.\n\n**Well-definedness.** The affine-space definition supplies the action and the\nunique displacements; the Lorentz-form definition supplies $\\eta$ and its\ninertia; the determinant-line definition supplies orientations; and the two\ncomponents of $T$ between which the time-orientation definition chooses exist by\nthe components lemma recorded in the well-definedness clause of\n[[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]], which is also what makes the\nnames future and past causal in item 4 well defined.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a $4$-dimensional real vector space ([[def-vector-space]],\n[[def-dimension]]). A **Lorentz form** on $V$ is a symmetric bilinear form\n$\\eta:V\\times V\\to\\mathbb R$ ([[def-bilinear-symmetric-skew-and-alternating-forms]])\nsuch that\n\n1. $\\eta$ is nondegenerate: both radicals\n   $\\operatorname{rad}_L(\\eta)=\\{u:\\eta(u,v)=0\\text{ for every }v\\}$ and\n   $\\operatorname{rad}_R(\\eta)=\\{v:\\eta(u,v)=0\\text{ for every }u\\}$ are\n   $\\{0\\}$ ([[def-matrix-radicals-rank-and-nondegeneracy-of-a-bilinear-form]]);\n   and\n2. $\\eta$ has **inertia** $(3,1,0)$: in some (equivalently, in every) basis of\n   $V$ in which the matrix of $\\eta$ is diagonal, exactly three diagonal\n   entries are positive, exactly one is negative, and none is zero\n   ([[def-definiteness-inertia-and-signature-data-over-the-reals]]).\n\nThe inertia of a symmetric bilinear form is the triple $(p,q,r)$ of numbers of\npositive, negative and zero diagonal entries of any diagonalizing basis, and it\nis independent of the diagonalizing basis by Sylvester's law of inertia\n([[def-definiteness-inertia-and-signature-data-over-the-reals]]); hence clause 2\nis a property of $\\eta$ itself and not of a chosen basis. The **associated\nquadratic form** of $\\eta$ is\n\n$$q(v):=\\eta(v,v),\\qquad v\\in V .$$\n\nA **Lorentz vector space of signature $(-,+,+,+)$** is a pair $(V,\\eta)$\nconsisting of a $4$-dimensional real vector space $V$ and a Lorentz form $\\eta$\non $V$; the name records that the diagonal normal form of $\\eta$ has one\nnegative entry followed by three positive entries. No positive-definite\nstructure on $V$ is assumed or implied by this definition, and $q$ takes both\nnegative and positive values on nonzero vectors: a basis in which\n$\\eta$ is diagonal with diagonal entries $-1,1,1,1$ exists by clause 2, and on\nits first vector $q$ is $-1$, while on each of the other three it is $+1$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(V,\\eta)$ be a Lorentz vector space of signature $(-,+,+,+)$\n([[def-lorentz-form-and-inertia]]), with causal terminology as in\n[[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]], and let\n\n$$T:=\\{v\\in V:\\eta(v,v)<0\\}$$\n\nbe its **timelike set**. Components and closures refer to the topology obtained\nby using any ordered basis to identify $V$ with Euclidean $\\mathbb R^4$;\n[[lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components]], step 5.1, proves that the\nresulting open sets are independent of the basis. By that lemma the\nset $T$ has exactly two connected components, and each of them is nonempty,\nopen, convex and path-connected. A **time orientation** of $(V,\\eta)$ is a\nchoice of one of these two components. Fixing such a choice, write $C^+$ for the\nchosen component and\n\n$$C^-:=T\\setminus C^+$$\n\nfor the other component; thus $T=C^+\\sqcup C^-$ is a disjoint union and\n$C^+$, $C^-$ are exactly the two components supplied by the lemma. Relative to\nthis choice a vector $v\\in V$ is\n\n- **future timelike** when $v\\in C^+$;\n- **past timelike** when $v\\in C^-$;\n- **future null** when $v\\neq 0$, $\\eta(v,v)=0$ and $v\\in\\operatorname{cl}(C^+)$;\n- **past null** when $v\\neq 0$, $\\eta(v,v)=0$ and $v\\in\\operatorname{cl}(C^-)$;\n- **future causal** when it is future timelike or future null;\n- **past causal** when it is past timelike or past null.\n\nThe sets $\\operatorname{cl}(C^+)$ and $\\operatorname{cl}(C^-)$ are the\n**future closed cone** and the **past closed cone** of the time-oriented space\n$(V,\\eta,C^+)$. By [[lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components]] each of them\nis a convex cone, their intersection is $\\{0\\}$, and a vector is causal exactly\nwhen it is future causal or past causal, in which case exactly one of these two\nalternatives holds: no nonzero vector is both future causal and past causal.\nThe timelike vectors are exactly the points of $T=C^+\\cup C^-$, and\n$0\\notin T$ because $\\eta(0,0)=0$; since a null vector is nonzero with\n$\\eta(v,v)=0$, no vector is both timelike and null, so the classes above are\nmutually exclusive where the definitions say they are: no vector is both future\nand past causal, and no timelike vector is null.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(V,\\eta)$ be a Lorentz vector space of signature $(-,+,+,+)$\n([[def-lorentz-form-and-inertia]]), with associated quadratic form\n$q(v)=\\eta(v,v)$. A vector $v\\in V$ is\n\n- **timelike** when $q(v)<0$;\n- **null**, or **lightlike**, when $v\\neq 0$ and $q(v)=0$;\n- **spacelike** when $q(v)>0$;\n- **causal** when it is timelike or null.\n\nThe zero vector $0\\in V$ satisfies $q(0)=0$ and is consequently **none** of\ntimelike, null or spacelike: the null case explicitly excludes it, and the\ntimelike and spacelike cases exclude it by $q(0)=0$ together with the strict\ninequalities. The **causal character** of a nonzero vector $v$ is the sign of\nthe real number $q(v)$, and the three names are attached to the three signs as\nabove. When no confusion arises, the pairing $\\eta$ itself is written\n$\\langle v,w\\rangle:=\\eta(v,w)$.\n\n**Each nonzero vector has exactly one causal character.** For a nonzero\n$v\\in V$ the real number $q(v)$ satisfies exactly one of $q(v)<0$, $q(v)=0$,\n$q(v)>0$ by trichotomy of the order on $\\mathbb R$, so $v$ is timelike, null or\nspacelike, and no two of the three alternatives can hold at once. The causal\nvectors are therefore exactly the nonzero vectors that are timelike or null,\nand the zero vector is causal in no sense introduced here.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-lebesgue-measurable-functions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let\n$U,V \\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ be open and let $T : U \\to V$ be a\n$C^1$ diffeomorphism. For every nonnegative Lebesgue measurable\n$f : V \\to [0,\\infty]$,\n$$ \\int_V f(y)\\,d\\lambda_n(y) = \\int_U f(T(x))\\,|\\det DT(x)|\\,d\\lambda_n(x). $$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "claim": "On $\\mathbf k\\ne0$ if $\\mu=0$, and on all $\\mathbf k$ if $\\mu>0$, $F_\\mu$ is a smooth chart of the shell. On every compact subset of this chart the density $[(2\\pi)^3 2\\omega_\\mu]^{-1}$ is bounded; for $\\mu=0$ near the omitted apex it is proportional to $|\\mathbf k|^{-1}$, whose integral over a radius-$r$ ball is proportional to $\\int_0^r s\\,ds<\\infty$. Compact annuli, and for $\\mu>0$ compact balls, give a countable cover by finite-measure sets; hence $d\\nu_\\mu$ is locally finite and sigma-finite.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "claim": "Fix $\\Lambda$ and write $F=F_\\mu$, $F(\\mathbf k')=\\Lambda F(\\mathbf k)$.\nBecause $\\Lambda$ preserves the future shell, this defines a smooth\ndiffeomorphism $\\mathbf k\\mapsto\\mathbf k'$ on the chart; let\n$J=D\\mathbf k'/D\\mathbf k$. Put $q_0=(1,0,0,0)$. The columns of\n$[q_0,DF(\\mathbf k)]$ have determinant $1$. Differentiating\n$\\Lambda F(\\mathbf k)=F(\\mathbf k')$ gives\n$\\Lambda DF(\\mathbf k)=DF(\\mathbf k')J$, and therefore\n$$1=\\det[\\Lambda q_0,DF(\\mathbf k')]\\det J.$$\nFor any $u\\in\\mathbb R^4$, direct expansion of the determinant gives\n$\\det[u,DF(\\mathbf k')]=-\\eta(u,F(\\mathbf k'))/\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k')$.\nTake $u=\\Lambda q_0$. Metric preservation and\n$F(\\mathbf k')=\\Lambda F(\\mathbf k)$ imply\n$-\\eta(\\Lambda q_0,F(\\mathbf k'))=-\\eta(q_0,F(\\mathbf k))\n=\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)$. Thus $\\det J=\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k')/\n\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)>0$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-3-1",
          "claim": "The change-of-variables theorem [F3] and the Jacobian from 2.1 give\n$$\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k'}{\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k')} =\\frac{\\det J\\,d^3\\mathbf k}{\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k')} =\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)}.$$\nFor the pullback $g(\\Lambda^{-1}p)$ in the Statement, apply the same\ncalculation to $\\Lambda^{-1}$, which is again proper orthochronous.\nMultiplication by the constant $[2(2\\pi)^3]^{-1}$ then proves invariance for\nnonnegative Borel functions. The massless apex is a\nsingle omitted point and was never in the chart; the locally finite density\nhas no extra point mass there.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
