    "lem-qft-mass-shell-test-range-dense": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-rational-box-step-functions-form-a-countable-dense-subset-of-l-p-of-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice.\n\nLet $1 \\le p < \\infty$. The finite linear combinations of indicator functions of\nhalf-open boxes with rational endpoints and rational coefficients form a\ncountable dense subset of $L^p(\\mathbb{R}^n)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-smooth-compactly-supported-functions-are-dense-in-ltwo-of-an-open-set",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open with $n\\ge1$ and $\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$. Then $C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$ is dense in $L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$: for every $f\\in L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$ and every $\\delta>0$ there is $\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$ with $\\|\\varphi-f\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}<\\delta$. Consequently $H^1_0(\\Omega)$ is dense in $L^2(\\Omega)$, and if a class $h\\in L^2(\\Omega)$ satisfies $(h,v)_{L^2}=0$ for all $v\\in H^1_0(\\Omega)$, then $h=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Here\n$\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ is the Schwartz space of smooth complex functions\nwhose coordinate-weighted derivatives are all bounded, as defined in\n[[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]].\nFix an oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski space and an inertial coordinate\nchart as in [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]. Use coordinates\n$x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$ and metric $\\eta=\\mathrm{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, matching the\n$(-+++)$ convention of the SR suppliers. Some QFT sources use its negative\n$(+---)$; the Lorentz group is unchanged, and the phase is fixed explicitly\nbelow.\nCoordinates $x$ have length units, momenta have inverse-length units, and the\nFourier phase below is dimensionless. A parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ has inverse-length\nunits and is only a mathematical shell parameter; it is not identified with a\nphysical rest mass. For $\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3$, set\n$$\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2},\\qquad p_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k).$$\nThe future mass shell is\n$$H_\\mu^+=\\{(p^0,\\mathbf p): (p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2=\\mu^2,\\ p^0>0\\}.$$\nWhen $\\mu=0$, its apex is omitted; thus $\\mathbf k\\ne0$ in the massless\nparameterization. The scalar shell pairing is\n$$p\\circ x:=p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x=-\\eta(p,x).$$\nDefine the measure and one-particle Hilbert space by\n$$d\\nu_\\mu(p_\\mu(\\mathbf k))= \\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)},\\qquad H_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu;\\mathbb C),$$\nwhere the omitted massless apex has measure zero. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb\nR^4)$ define its pointwise shell Fourier restriction by\n$$K_\\mu f(p)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}f(x)e^{ip\\circ x}\\,d^4x.$$\nThe Fourier convention is exactly the displayed integral, with no prefactor.\nIf $\\widehat f(\\xi^0,\\boldsymbol\\xi)=\n\\int f(x)e^{-2\\pi i(x^0\\xi^0+\\mathbf x\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\xi)}\\,d^4x$ denotes\nthe standard transform in\n[[cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space]],\nthen\n$$\\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p):=\\int f(x)e^{i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)}\\,d^4x =\\widehat f\\!\\left(-\\frac{p^0}{2\\pi},\\frac{\\mathbf p}{2\\pi}\\right).$$\nIts inverse is\n$$f(x)=(2\\pi)^{-4}\\int_{\\mathbb R^4} \\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p)e^{-i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)} \\,dp^0\\,d^3\\mathbf p.$$\nThus this convention is a time reflection and rescaling of the cited\nSchwartz automorphism and has the stated reciprocal normalization.\nThe proper orthochronous Lorentz group consists of linear maps preserving\n$\\eta$, orientation and the chosen future cone. A Poincare map is\n$x\\mapsto a+\\Lambda x$ with $a\\in\\mathbb R^4$ and $\\Lambda$ in that group.\n\nThis is a mathematical free scalar one-particle construction. It adopts the\nchosen coordinates, measure and Fourier convention; it asserts no experimental\nobservation, physical mass identification or measurement outcome.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. The Fourier transform is a topological automorphism of $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^n)$, with $\\mathcal F^2=R$ and $\\mathcal F^{-1}=R\\mathcal F$, where $Rf(x)=f(-x)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $0<r<R$ and $n\\ge 1$. Then there exists a smooth function $\\rho:\\mathbb R^n\\to [0,1]$ such that $\\rho=1$ on $\\overline B_r(0)$ and $\\operatorname{supp}(\\rho)\\subseteq B_R(0)$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $0\\le f_1\\le f_2\\le\\cdots$ be measurable and suppose $f_n(x)\\uparrow f(x)$\nfor every $x$. Then\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\uparrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "In the chart $\\mathbf k\\mapsto p_\\mu(\\mathbf k)$, write\n$w_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=[2(2\\pi)^3\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)]^{-1}$. By [F3] and the\nfixed invertible reflection and rescaling in\n[[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], $\\mathcal F_+f$ is Schwartz for every\n$f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$. Thus\n$$q_2(f):=\\sup_{q\\in\\mathbb R^4}(1+|q|)^2|\\mathcal F_+f(q)|<\\infty.$$\nOn the shell, $|\\mathcal F_+f(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k)|\\le\nq_2(f)(1+|\\mathbf k|)^{-2}$, so\n$$\\|K_\\mu f\\|_{H_\\mu}^2\\le\\frac{q_2(f)^2}{2(2\\pi)^3}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{(1+|\\mathbf k|)^{-4}}{\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)}\\,d^3\\mathbf k<\\infty.$$\nThe radial integrand is $O(r)$ near zero if $\\mu=0$, $O(r^2)$ near zero if\n$\\mu>0$, and $O(r^{-3})$ at infinity. Therefore every $K_\\mu f$ lies in\n$H_\\mu$, so the claimed range is a subset of the Hilbert space. By [F1],\nreal-valued $L^2(\\mathbb R^3)$ has a countable dense subset $S_{\\mathbb R}$.\nFor complex-valued $h=u+iv\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C)$, its real and\nimaginary parts lie in real-valued $L^2$. For any $\\varepsilon>0$, choose\n$s,t\\in S_{\\mathbb R}$ with $\\|u-s\\|_2,\\|v-t\\|_2<\\varepsilon/\\sqrt2$.\nThen $\\|h-(s+it)\\|_2^2=\\|u-s\\|_2^2+\\|v-t\\|_2^2<\\varepsilon^2$.\nEnumerate $S_{\\mathbb R}$ by $(s_j)_{j\\in\\mathbb N_0}$, repeating terms if\nit is finite. The Cantor pairing map\n$(j,k)\\mapsto (j+k)(j+k+1)/2+k$ enumerates $\\mathbb N_0^2$, so\n$S_{\\mathbb R}+iS_{\\mathbb R}$ is countable and dense in complex-valued\n$L^2(\\mathbb R^3)$. Multiplication by $\\sqrt{w_\\mu}$ is an isometry from\n$H_\\mu$ onto $L^2(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\},d^3\\mathbf k;\\mathbb C)$ (for\n$\\mu=0$) or $L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3\\mathbf k;\\mathbb C)$ (for $\\mu>0$), with\ninverse division by $\\sqrt{w_\\mu}$. In the massless case, extend any function\non the punctured space by zero at the null point, approximate it by the\ncountable dense family just constructed, and restrict those approximants back\nto the punctured space; restriction does not increase the norm. Thus both\nchart spaces, and therefore $H_\\mu$, are separable.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Let $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^3$ for $\\mu>0$ and\n$\\Omega=\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$ for $\\mu=0$. For\n$\\Omega_n=\\{1/n<|\\mathbf k|<n\\}$, these sets increase to $\\Omega\\setminus\\{0\\}$; its complement in $\\Omega$ is null for the shell measure.\nFor $h\\in L^2(\\Omega,w_\\mu d^3\\mathbf k)$, monotone convergence [F5] gives\n$$\\int_{\\Omega_n}|h|^2w_\\mu\\,d^3\\mathbf k\\longrightarrow\n\\int_\\Omega|h|^2w_\\mu\\,d^3\\mathbf k<\\infty.$$\nSubtracting these finite integrals from the total shows\n$\\|h\\mathbf1_{\\Omega\\setminus\\Omega_n}\\|_{L^2(w_\\mu)}\\to0$.\nOn each $\\Omega_n$, $w_\\mu$ is smooth and bounded above and below by\npositive constants. Approximate $h|_{\\Omega_n}$ in ordinary $L^2(\\Omega_n)$\nby a function in $C_c^\\infty(\\Omega_n)$ using [F2]; equivalence of the\nweighted and ordinary norms on $\\Omega_n$ gives weighted approximation.\nThus $C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$ is dense in the shell space.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Fix $\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$. Choose $\\rho\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R)$\nwith $\\rho(0)=1$, using [F4], and put\n$$G(q^0,\\mathbf q)= \\psi(\\mathbf q)\\rho(q^0-\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf q)).$$\nBecause $\\psi$ has compact support away from the massless apex, $\\omega_\\mu$\nis smooth on a neighborhood of its support, so this is a smooth compactly\nsupported function on $\\mathbb R^4$. By the Fourier automorphism [F3] there\nis $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ with\n$\\mathcal F_+f(q^0,\\mathbf q)=G(q^0,\\mathbf q)$ in the convention of\n[[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. On the shell,\n$K_\\mu f(p_\\mu(\\mathbf k))=G(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k)\n=\\psi(\\mathbf k)$. Hence the test restriction range contains\n$C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$, which is dense by 2.1.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Density of Schwartz test restrictions in the scalar shell space: Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. For every $\\mu\\ge0$, the space $H_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu)$ of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]] is separable (has a countable norm-dense subset), and $$\\{K_\\mu f:f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)\\}$$ is dense in $H_\\mu$. If $\\mu>0$, the range contains every function in $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$; if $\\mu=0$, it contains every function in $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\})$. The specified witness, fixed coordinate space or supplied Hilbert/test objects are the inputs of this assertion; it does not assert a separate existence result after replacing them by an empty underlying set."
