          "source": "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix an integer $n\\ge1$. With the complex smooth-function convention of [[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]] and multi-indices of [[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]], define\n$$p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)=\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R^n}|x^\\alpha\\partial^\\beta f(x)|,\\qquad \\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^n)=\\{f\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C):p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)<\\infty\\text{ for all }\\alpha,\\beta\\}.$$\nThe zero multi-index gives $x^0=1$ and $\\partial^0f=f$. These are actual smooth functions, not equivalence classes. The notation $C_c^\\infty$ uses the support convention in [[def-c-c-and-c-c-infinity-on-rn]].\n\nPointwise differentiation is linear; thus $p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f+g)\\le p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)+p_{\\alpha\\beta}(g)$ and $p_{\\alpha\\beta}(cf)=|c|p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)$. In particular this set is a complex vector space and each $p_{\\alpha\\beta}$ is a seminorm. The zero function belongs to it. Since $p_{00}(f)=0$ forces $f(x)=0$ for every $x$, the family separates functions. No choice principle is needed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. The Fourier transform is a topological automorphism of $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^n)$, with $\\mathcal F^2=R$ and $\\mathcal F^{-1}=R\\mathcal F$, where $Rf(x)=f(-x)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-fourier-transform-maps-schwartz-space-continuously-to-itself",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. The negative-sign, $2\\pi$-normalized Fourier transform is continuous $\\mathcal F:\\mathcal S\\to\\mathcal S$, and\n$$\\mathcal F(\\partial^\\alpha f)(\\xi)=(2\\pi i\\xi)^\\alpha\\widehat f(\\xi),\\qquad \\partial^\\beta\\widehat f=\\mathcal F((-2\\pi ix)^\\beta f).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "claim": "Define the continuous Schwartz seminorm\n$$q_2(f)=\\sup_{p\\in\\mathbb R^4}(1+|p|)^2|\\mathcal F_+f(p)|.$$\nContinuity follows from [F3,F4] and [F2]. On shell,\n$|\\mathcal F_+f(p_\\mu(\\mathbf k))|\n\\le q_2(f)(1+|\\mathbf k|)^{-2}$ because\n$|p_\\mu(\\mathbf k)|\\ge|\\mathbf k|$. Hence\n$$\\|K_\\mu f\\|_{H_\\mu}^2 \\le\\frac{q_2(f)^2}{2(2\\pi)^3} \\int_{\\mathbb R^3} \\frac{(1+|\\mathbf k|)^{-4}}{\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)}\\,d^3\\mathbf k.$$\nThe integral is finite: in polar coordinates its radial integrand is\n$r^2(1+r)^{-4}/\\sqrt{r^2+\\mu^2}$, which is $O(r)$ near $0$ when $\\mu=0$,\nis $O(r^2)$ near $0$ when $\\mu>0$, and is $O(r^{-3})$ at infinity.\nTherefore $K_\\mu f\\in H_\\mu$, and the displayed seminorm bound proves\ncontinuity and linearity.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "claim": "By [F4], applying $\\partial_0^2-\\Delta+\\mu^2$ before $\\mathcal F_+$\nmultiplies by $-(p^0)^2+|\\mathbf p|^2+\\mu^2$. On the shell this multiplier\nis $-\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)^2+|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2=0$. Restriction to the\nshell therefore gives the asserted Klein–Gordon identity.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
