        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Unitary lift to bosonic Fock space: Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $H$ and $K$ be complex Hilbert spaces and let $U:H\\to K$ be unitary. The sector maps $U^{\\otimes n}$ restrict to unitaries $U_s^{(n)}:H_s^{(n)}\\to K_s^{(n)}$; their direct sum $$\\Gamma_s(U)=\\bigoplus_{n=0}^\\infty U_s^{(n)}$$ is unitary, maps vacuum to vacuum, and maps the finite-particle domain $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}(H)$ onto $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}(K)$. It intertwines the creation and annihilation maps: $$\\Gamma_s(U)a_H^\\dagger(h)\\Gamma_s(U)^{-1}=a_K^\\dagger(Uh),\\qquad \\Gamma_s(U)a_H(h)\\Gamma_s(U)^{-1}=a_K(Uh)$$ on the finite-particle domain. If $G$ is a topological space and $g\\mapsto U_g$ is a family of unitaries such that $g\\mapsto U_gh$ is norm-continuous for every $h\\in H$, then $g\\mapsto\\Gamma_s(U_g)$ has the same continuity property on $\\mathcal F_s(H)$. No additional closed-interval endpoint is quantified in this particular assertion. The given strict parameter inequalities and domain-qualified support/series identities are retained; this worksheet does not extend them across a forbidden endpoint."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and the declared premises of Unitary lift to bosonic Fock space retain the stated choice convention. Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $H$ and $K$ be complex Hilbert spaces and let $U:H\\to K$ be unitary. The sector maps $U^{\\otimes n}$ restrict to unitaries $U_s^{(n)}:H_s^{(n)}\\to K_s^{(n)}$; their direct sum $$\\Gamma_s(U)=\\bigoplus_{n=0}^\\infty U_s^{(n)}$$ is unitary, maps vacuum to vacuum, and maps the finite-particle domain $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}(H)$ onto $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}(K)$. It intertwines the creation and annihilation maps: $$\\Gamma_s(U)a_H^\\dagger(h)\\Gamma_s(U)^{-1}=a_K^\\dagger(Uh),\\qquad \\Gamma_s(U)a_H(h)\\Gamma_s(U)^{-1}=a_K(Uh)$$ on the finite-particle domain. If $g\\mapsto U_g$ is a strongly continuous family of unitaries, then $g\\mapsto\\Gamma_s(U_g)$ is strongly continuous. This record concerns that existing premise and the fixed supplier data; it asserts no stronger choice principle or unstated family selection."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Unitary lift to bosonic Fock space: Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $H$ and $K$ be complex Hilbert spaces and let $U:H\\to K$ be unitary. The sector maps $U^{\\otimes n}$ restrict to unitaries $U_s^{(n)}:H_s^{(n)}\\to K_s^{(n)}$; their direct sum $$\\Gamma_s(U)=\\bigoplus_{n=0}^\\infty U_s^{(n)}$$ is unitary, maps vacuum to vacuum, and maps the finite-particle domain $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}(H)$ onto $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}(K)$. It intertwines the creation and annihilation maps: $$\\Gamma_s(U)a_H^\\dagger(h)\\Gamma_s(U)^{-1}=a_K^\\dagger(Uh),\\qquad \\Gamma_s(U)a_H(h)\\Gamma_s(U)^{-1}=a_K(Uh)$$ on the finite-particle domain. If $G$ is a topological space and $g\\mapsto U_g$ is a family of unitaries such that $g\\mapsto U_gh$ is norm-continuous for every $h\\in H$, then $g\\mapsto\\Gamma_s(U_g)$ has the same continuity property on $\\mathcal F_s(H)$. The forward iff axis adds no separately stated converse hypothesis here; the recorded claim is the displayed construction, identity, implication or scope qualification, not a characterization by a new necessary-and-sufficient condition."
        },
