        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Strongly continuous scalar one-particle Poincare action: Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Use the proper orthochronous Poincare group $G=\\mathbb R^4\\rtimes SO^+(1,3)$ in the coordinates of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. Give $SO^+(1,3)$ the matrix subspace topology from $M_4(\\mathbb R)$ and $G$ the product topology on $\\mathbb R^4\\times SO^+(1,3)$, with the group law below. A unitary representation of this topological group is a homomorphism into the unitary operators on $H_\\mu$; it is strongly continuous when each orbit map $g\\mapsto U_\\mu(g)h$ is norm-continuous for $h\\in H_\\mu$. For $g=(a,\\Lambda)\\in G$, define $$f_g(x)=f(\\Lambda^{-1}(x-a)),\\qquad [U_\\mu(g)h](p)=e^{ip\\circ a}h(\\Lambda^{-1}p).$$ Then $f_g\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$, $U_\\mu(g)$ is a well-defined unitary on $H_\\mu$, $g\\mapsto U_\\mu(g)$ is a strongly continuous representation, and $$K_\\mu f_g=U_\\mu(g)K_\\mu f \\qquad(f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)).$$ The group product convention is $(a,\\Lambda)(b,\\Gamma)=(a+\\Lambda b,\\Lambda\\Gamma)$. The objects have the fixed fiber, test domain, witness or shell specified there. This claim does not vary a finite set cardinality through a singleton boundary; any displayed unit scalar/vector is part of the stated data."
