    "lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1",
            "3.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For an integer $d\\ge1$, let $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$ be the complex Schwartz space with seminorms\n$p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)=\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R^d}|x^\\alpha\\partial^\\beta f(x)|$\nfor all multi-indices $\\alpha,\\beta$; this is exactly\n[[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]]. Let\n$\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^d)=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ with the support convention of\n[[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]] and the locally convex LF topology of\n[[def-test-function-topology]]. The tempered-distribution space $\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^d)$\nis the space of continuous complex-linear functionals on this topological\n$\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$, paired bilinearly with test functions, as in\n[[def-tempered-distribution]].\n\nFor every complex Hilbert space $H$, use the second-variable-linear pairing\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_2:=(v,u)_1$, where $(\\cdot,\\cdot)_1$ is the first-variable-linear\nHilbert pairing of [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]. Thus\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_2$ is linear in $v$ and conjugate-linear in $u$.\nFor each tensor product, use the same norm completion as\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]], but equip it with the\nsecond-variable-linear pairing obtained by conjugating that supplier's\nfirst-variable-linear pairing. This leaves the norm and completion unchanged;\non elementary tensors it gives the product of the second-variable pairings.\nLet $H^{\\widehat\\otimes n}$ be the iterated Hilbert tensor completion, with\n$H^{\\widehat\\otimes0}:=\\mathbb C$; the tensor inner product is the product of\nthe factor pairings. Identify parenthesizations by the isometry fixing\nelementary tensors: the product pairing is the same on their dense span,\nso the map and its inverse extend to the completions. For $n\\ge1$, let\n$P_{s,n}=n!^{-1}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}U_\\pi$ and\n$P_{a,n}=n!^{-1}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\pi)U_\\pi$, using the\npermutation action and the orthogonal projections of\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections]]. Define\n$H_s^{(n)}=\\operatorname{ran}P_{s,n}$ and\n$H_a^{(n)}=\\operatorname{ran}P_{a,n}$, with\n$H_s^{(0)}=H_a^{(0)}=\\mathbb C$. These are the symmetric and antisymmetric\n$n$-particle sectors.\n\nThe bosonic and fermionic Fock Hilbert spaces are the square-summable direct\nsums\n$\\mathcal F_s(H)=\\{(\\Psi_n)_{n\\ge0}:\\Psi_n\\in H_s^{(n)},\\\n\\sum_n\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}$ and\n$\\mathcal F_a(H)=\\{(\\Psi_n)_{n\\ge0}:\\Psi_n\\in H_a^{(n)},\\\n\\sum_n\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}$, with the componentwise inner product.\nTheir vacuum is $\\Omega=(1,0,0,\\ldots)$. The finite-particle subspace\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ consists of sequences with only finitely many nonzero\ncomponents.\n\nFor $h\\in H$, the map $C_h:H\\to\\mathbb C$, $C_h(v)=\\langle h,v\\rangle_2$, is linear and bounded:\n$|C_h(v)|=|(v,h)_1|\\le\\|h\\|\\,\\|v\\|$ by\n[[thm-cauchy-schwarz-for-real-and-complex-inner-product-spaces]].\nFor any Hilbert space $K$, identify $\\mathbb C\\widehat\\otimes K$ with $K$\nby $J_K(z\\otimes v)=zv$. Every algebraic tensor is $1\\otimes w$;\nthe tensor norm is then $\\|w\\|$, so $J_K$ extends to a surjective isometry\non the completion (with inverse $w\\mapsto1\\otimes w$).\nThroughout the following formulas, $C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)}$ means\n$J_{H^{\\widehat\\otimes(n-1)}}\\circ(C_h\\widehat\\otimes I)$, with\n$I$ the identity on $H^{\\widehat\\otimes(n-1)}$.\nThe bounded tensor-map supplier therefore applies and gives norm at most\n$\\|h\\|$. This includes $n=1$, where $H^{\\widehat\\otimes0}=\\mathbb C$.\nContraction commutes with permutations of the remaining $n-1$ factors,\nso it takes symmetric or antisymmetric tensors to the corresponding\n$(n-1)$-particle sector (and to $\\mathbb C$ for $n=1$).\n\nFor $n\\ge0$, bosonic creation on sector $n$ is\n$a_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}P_{s,n+1}(h\\otimes\\Psi_n)$; for $n\\ge1$,\nbosonic annihilation is\n$a_n(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt n(C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)})\\Psi_n$, where\n$C_h$ is the bounded functional above, its tensor extension is supplied by\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-bounded-tensor-maps]], and $a(h)\\Omega=0$. Define fermionic creation\nby $c_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}P_{a,n+1}(h\\otimes\\Psi_n)$; for $n\\ge1$,\nfermionic annihilation is the corresponding first-factor contraction\n$c_n(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt n(C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)})\\Psi_n$, and\n$c(h)\\Omega=0$. The annihilation maps are conjugate-linear in $h$; the\ncreation maps are linear in $h$. These formulas define sector maps on the\nfinite-particle subspace; their maximal closed domains and operator relations are proved in\n[[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. No physical statistics assignment or spin–statistics\ntheorem is part of these mathematical definitions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] and let $H$ be a complex\nHilbert space with the second-variable-linear convention of\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. On $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ define the bosonic\ncreation and annihilation maps $a^\\dagger(h)$ and $a(h)$ and the fermionic maps\n$c^\\dagger(h)$ and $c(h)$ as in\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. Write $a_n(h)$ and $a_n^\\dagger(h)$ for\ntheir restrictions to the $n$th bosonic sector. For bosons set\n\n$$D(a(h))=\\{\\Psi\\in\\mathcal F_s(H):\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\|a_n(h)\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\},\\qquad D(a^\\dagger(h))=\\{\\Psi\\in\\mathcal F_s(H):\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\|a_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}.$$\n\nThen $\\mathcal F_s(H)$ and $\\mathcal F_a(H)$ are Hilbert spaces,\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is dense, and it is a graph core for both maximal\nbosonic sector maps. The bosonic operators on their displayed domains are\nclosed and mutually adjoint, and on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ satisfy\n\n$$[a(h),a^\\dagger(k)]=\\langle h,k\\rangle_2I,\\qquad [a(h),a(k)]=[a^\\dagger(h),a^\\dagger(k)]=0.$$\n\nThe fermionic $c(h)$ and $c^\\dagger(h)$ extend to bounded operators on the\nwhole Fock space, with norms $\\|h\\|$, and satisfy on the whole space\n\n$$\\{c(h),c^\\dagger(k)\\}=\\langle h,k\\rangle_2I,\\qquad \\{c(h),c(k)\\}=\\{c^\\dagger(h),c^\\dagger(k)\\}=0.$$\n\nThis is a mathematical construction of the two Fock representations; it does\nnot assign particle statistics to a physical species.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $H$ be a separable\ncomplex Hilbert space with the second-variable-linear convention of\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]], and let $\\mathcal F_s(H)$ and\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ be its bosonic Fock space and finite-particle domain.\nFor $h\\in H$ define on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ the Segal field\n$$\\phi_0(h):=2^{-1/2}\\bigl(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h)\\bigr).$$\nFor $h\\ne0$, this symmetric operator is essentially self-adjoint; write\n$\\Phi(h)=\\overline{\\phi_0(h)}$. If $r=\\|h\\|$, $e=h/r$, $K=e^\\perp$,\n$G=\\mathcal F_s(K)$, choose a countable orthonormal basis $(u_j)$ of $G$\nwhose vectors are finite-particle, and use the one-mode splitting of\n[[lem-qft-fock-one-mode-splitting]] followed by the Hermite unitary\n$|n\\rangle\\mapsto g_n$ into $L^2(\\mathbb R,dx;\\mathbb C)$ with pairing\n$\\langle f,g\\rangle_2=\\int\\overline f g$ to write a vector as\n$(f_j)_{j\\in J}\\in\\ell^2(J;L^2(\\mathbb R,dx;\\mathbb C))$. Then the exact domain and\naction are\n$$D(\\Phi(h))=\\left\\{(f_j):\\sum_{j\\in J}\\int_{\\mathbb R}(1+r^2x^2)|f_j(x)|^2\\,dx<\\infty\\right\\},\\qquad (\\Phi(h)f)_j(x)=rx f_j(x).$$\nFor $h=0$, $\\phi_0(0)$ closes to the zero operator on all of $\\mathcal F_s(H)$.\n\nDefine $\\epsilon(z)=\\bigoplus_{n\\ge0}z^{\\otimes n}/\\sqrt{n!}$ for $z\\in H$.\nThere are unitaries $W(h)$ determined on these total exponential vectors by\n$$W(h)\\epsilon(z)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\|h\\|^2}{4} +\\frac{i}{\\sqrt2}\\langle h,z\\rangle_2\\right) \\epsilon\\!\\left(z+\\frac{i h}{\\sqrt2}\\right).$$\nThey obey\n$$W(h)W(k)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{i}{2}\\operatorname{Im}\\langle h,k\\rangle_2\\right)W(h+k),\\qquad \\langle\\Omega,W(h)\\Omega\\rangle_2=e^{-\\|h\\|^2/4},$$\nand $h\\mapsto W(h)$ is strongly continuous. For every $h$, the one-parameter\ngroup $t\\mapsto W(th)$ has norm derivative at zero exactly on\n$D(\\Phi(h))$, with derivative $i\\Phi(h)$ (for $h=0$ this is the zero\ngenerator). Thus the displayed bounded Weyl operators have precisely the\nclosed Segal fields as their generators; no point-field or measurement claim\nis included.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Here\n$\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ is the Schwartz space of smooth complex functions\nwhose coordinate-weighted derivatives are all bounded, as defined in\n[[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]].\nFix an oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski space and an inertial coordinate\nchart as in [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]. Use coordinates\n$x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$ and metric $\\eta=\\mathrm{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, matching the\n$(-+++)$ convention of the SR suppliers. Some QFT sources use its negative\n$(+---)$; the Lorentz group is unchanged, and the phase is fixed explicitly\nbelow.\nCoordinates $x$ have length units, momenta have inverse-length units, and the\nFourier phase below is dimensionless. A parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ has inverse-length\nunits and is only a mathematical shell parameter; it is not identified with a\nphysical rest mass. For $\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3$, set\n$$\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2},\\qquad p_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k).$$\nThe future mass shell is\n$$H_\\mu^+=\\{(p^0,\\mathbf p): (p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2=\\mu^2,\\ p^0>0\\}.$$\nWhen $\\mu=0$, its apex is omitted; thus $\\mathbf k\\ne0$ in the massless\nparameterization. The scalar shell pairing is\n$$p\\circ x:=p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x=-\\eta(p,x).$$\nDefine the measure and one-particle Hilbert space by\n$$d\\nu_\\mu(p_\\mu(\\mathbf k))= \\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)},\\qquad H_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu;\\mathbb C),$$\nwhere the omitted massless apex has measure zero. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb\nR^4)$ define its pointwise shell Fourier restriction by\n$$K_\\mu f(p)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}f(x)e^{ip\\circ x}\\,d^4x.$$\nThe Fourier convention is exactly the displayed integral, with no prefactor.\nIf $\\widehat f(\\xi^0,\\boldsymbol\\xi)=\n\\int f(x)e^{-2\\pi i(x^0\\xi^0+\\mathbf x\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\xi)}\\,d^4x$ denotes\nthe standard transform in\n[[cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space]],\nthen\n$$\\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p):=\\int f(x)e^{i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)}\\,d^4x =\\widehat f\\!\\left(-\\frac{p^0}{2\\pi},\\frac{\\mathbf p}{2\\pi}\\right).$$\nIts inverse is\n$$f(x)=(2\\pi)^{-4}\\int_{\\mathbb R^4} \\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p)e^{-i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)} \\,dp^0\\,d^3\\mathbf p.$$\nThus this convention is a time reflection and rescaling of the cited\nSchwartz automorphism and has the stated reciprocal normalization.\nThe proper orthochronous Lorentz group consists of linear maps preserving\n$\\eta$, orientation and the chosen future cone. A Poincare map is\n$x\\mapsto a+\\Lambda x$ with $a\\in\\mathbb R^4$ and $\\Lambda$ in that group.\n\nThis is a mathematical free scalar one-particle construction. It adopts the\nchosen coordinates, measure and Fourier convention; it asserts no experimental\nobservation, physical mass identification or measurement outcome.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-qft-mass-shell-measure-invariant",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. For every $\\mu\\ge0$, the measure $d\\nu_\\mu$ of\n[[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]] is locally finite and sigma-finite on $H_\\mu^+$.\nFor every proper orthochronous Lorentz transformation $\\Lambda$ and every\nnonnegative Borel function $g$ on $H_\\mu^+$,\n$$\\int_{H_\\mu^+}g(\\Lambda^{-1}p)\\,d\\nu_\\mu(p) =\\int_{H_\\mu^+}g(p)\\,d\\nu_\\mu(p).$$\nThe claim applies off the omitted apex when $\\mu=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-qft-mass-shell-test-range-dense",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. For every $\\mu\\ge0$, the\nspace $H_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu)$ of\n[[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]] is separable (has a countable norm-dense subset), and\n$$\\{K_\\mu f:f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)\\}$$\nis dense in $H_\\mu$. If $\\mu>0$, the range contains every function in\n$C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$; if $\\mu=0$, it contains every function in\n$C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\})$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-qft-fock-unitary-lift",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $H$ and $K$ be complex\nHilbert spaces and let $U:H\\to K$ be unitary. The sector maps\n$U^{\\otimes n}$ restrict to unitaries $U_s^{(n)}:H_s^{(n)}\\to K_s^{(n)}$;\ntheir direct sum\n$$\\Gamma_s(U)=\\bigoplus_{n=0}^\\infty U_s^{(n)}$$\nis unitary, maps vacuum to vacuum, and maps the finite-particle domain\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}(H)$ onto $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}(K)$. It intertwines the\ncreation and annihilation maps:\n$$\\Gamma_s(U)a_H^\\dagger(h)\\Gamma_s(U)^{-1}=a_K^\\dagger(Uh),\\qquad \\Gamma_s(U)a_H(h)\\Gamma_s(U)^{-1}=a_K(Uh)$$\non the finite-particle domain. If $G$ is a topological space and $g\\mapsto U_g$\nis a family of unitaries such that $g\\mapsto U_gh$ is norm-continuous for\nevery $h\\in H$, then $g\\mapsto\\Gamma_s(U_g)$ has the same continuity\nproperty on $\\mathcal F_s(H)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let\n$(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a sigma-finite measure space and let\n$\\boldsymbol\\omega=(\\omega_1,\\ldots,\\omega_d):X\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be a\nfinite-valued measurable map, where $d\\ge1$. Put\n$H=L^2(X,\\mu;\\mathbb C)$ with the second-variable-linear pairing of\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]], and form the bosonic Fock space\n$\\mathcal F_s(H)$. For $n\\ge1$, identify $H_s^{(n)}$ with the symmetric\nsubspace of $L^2(X^n,\\overline{\\mu^{\\times n}};\\mathbb C)$ by iterating\n[[lem-qft-l2-product-tensor-identification]]. Set $X^0=\\{\\star\\}$,\n$s_0(\\star)=0$, and for $n\\ge1$ set\n$$s_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)=\\sum_{r=1}^n\\boldsymbol\\omega(x_r)\\in\\mathbb R^d.$$\nFor $1\\le j\\le d$, define $H_j$ by multiplication by the $j$th coordinate\n$s_{n,j}$ on sector $n$, with exact domain\n$$D(H_j)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n): \\sum_{n\\ge1}\\int_{X^n}|s_{n,j}|^2|\\Psi_n|^2\\,d\\overline{\\mu^{\\times n}}<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nEach $H_j$ is self-adjoint, and $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\cap D(H_j)$ is a\ngraph core. The joint projection-valued measure\n[[def-projection-valued-measure]] on $\\mathbb R^d$ is\n$$(E(B)\\Psi)_n=\\mathbf1_{s_n^{-1}(B)}\\Psi_n\\quad(n\\ge1),\\qquad (E(B)\\Psi)_0=\\mathbf1_B(0)\\Psi_0.$$\nFor $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R^d$, let $H_\\theta$ be multiplication on sector $n$\nby $\\theta\\cdot s_n$, with its maximal square-integrability domain. Then\n$$U_\\theta(t)\\Psi:=\\bigl(e^{it\\theta\\cdot s_n}\\Psi_n\\bigr)_{n\\ge0}$$\nis a strongly continuous unitary group on all of Fock space, and its norm\nderivative at zero exists exactly for $\\Psi\\in D(H_\\theta)$, where it is\n$iH_\\theta\\Psi$. The zero-particle sector is the vacuum eigenvector at joint\nvalue $0$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "lem-qft-mass-shell-test-restriction-continuity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. For every $\\mu\\ge0$, the\npointwise restriction\n$$K_\\mu f(p)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}f(x)e^{ip\\circ x}\\,d^4x$$\nof [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]] maps $\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ continuously\nand linearly into $H_\\mu$. It also annihilates the Klein–Gordon test operator:\n$$K_\\mu\\bigl((\\partial_0^2-\\Delta+\\mu^2)f\\bigr)=0 \\qquad(f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)).$$\nThe coordinates are the fixed length coordinates of that definition and\n$\\mu$ has inverse-length units.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "lem-qft-scalar-poincare-action",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Use the proper\northochronous Poincare group\n$G=\\mathbb R^4\\rtimes SO^+(1,3)$ in the coordinates of\n[[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. Give $SO^+(1,3)$ the matrix subspace topology\nfrom $M_4(\\mathbb R)$ and $G$ the product topology on\n$\\mathbb R^4\\times SO^+(1,3)$, with the group law below. A unitary\nrepresentation of this topological group is a homomorphism into the unitary\noperators on $H_\\mu$; it is strongly continuous when each orbit map\n$g\\mapsto U_\\mu(g)h$ is norm-continuous for $h\\in H_\\mu$. For\n$g=(a,\\Lambda)\\in G$, define\n$$f_g(x)=f(\\Lambda^{-1}(x-a)),\\qquad [U_\\mu(g)h](p)=e^{ip\\circ a}h(\\Lambda^{-1}p).$$\nThen $f_g\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$, $U_\\mu(g)$ is a well-defined unitary\non $H_\\mu$, $g\\mapsto U_\\mu(g)$ is a strongly continuous representation, and\n$$K_\\mu f_g=U_\\mu(g)K_\\mu f \\qquad(f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)).$$\nThe group product convention is\n$(a,\\Lambda)(b,\\Gamma)=(a+\\Lambda b,\\Lambda\\Gamma)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. The Fourier transform is a topological automorphism of $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^n)$, with $\\mathcal F^2=R$ and $\\mathcal F^{-1}=R\\mathcal F$, where $Rf(x)=f(-x)$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix an integer $n\\ge1$. With the complex smooth-function convention of [[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]] and multi-indices of [[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]], define\n$$p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)=\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R^n}|x^\\alpha\\partial^\\beta f(x)|,\\qquad \\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^n)=\\{f\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C):p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)<\\infty\\text{ for all }\\alpha,\\beta\\}.$$\nThe zero multi-index gives $x^0=1$ and $\\partial^0f=f$. These are actual smooth functions, not equivalence classes. The notation $C_c^\\infty$ uses the support convention in [[def-c-c-and-c-c-infinity-on-rn]].\n\nPointwise differentiation is linear; thus $p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f+g)\\le p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)+p_{\\alpha\\beta}(g)$ and $p_{\\alpha\\beta}(cf)=|c|p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)$. In particular this set is a complex vector space and each $p_{\\alpha\\beta}$ is a seminorm. The zero function belongs to it. Since $p_{00}(f)=0$ forces $f(x)=0$ for every $x$, the family separates functions. No choice principle is needed.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For an integer $d\\ge1$, let $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$ be the complex Schwartz space with seminorms\n$p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)=\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R^d}|x^\\alpha\\partial^\\beta f(x)|$\nfor all multi-indices $\\alpha,\\beta$; this is exactly\n[[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]]. Let\n$\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^d)=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ with the support convention of\n[[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]] and the locally convex LF topology of\n[[def-test-function-topology]]. The tempered-distribution space $\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^d)$\nis the space of continuous complex-linear functionals on this topological\n$\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$, paired bilinearly with test functions, as in\n[[def-tempered-distribution]].\n\nFor every complex Hilbert space $H$, use the second-variable-linear pairing\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_2:=(v,u)_1$, where $(\\cdot,\\cdot)_1$ is the first-variable-linear\nHilbert pairing of [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]. Thus\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_2$ is linear in $v$ and conjugate-linear in $u$.\nFor each tensor product, use the same norm completion as\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]], but equip it with the\nsecond-variable-linear pairing obtained by conjugating that supplier's\nfirst-variable-linear pairing. This leaves the norm and completion unchanged;\non elementary tensors it gives the product of the second-variable pairings.\nLet $H^{\\widehat\\otimes n}$ be the iterated Hilbert tensor completion, with\n$H^{\\widehat\\otimes0}:=\\mathbb C$; the tensor inner product is the product of\nthe factor pairings. Identify parenthesizations by the isometry fixing\nelementary tensors: the product pairing is the same on their dense span,\nso the map and its inverse extend to the completions. For $n\\ge1$, let\n$P_{s,n}=n!^{-1}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}U_\\pi$ and\n$P_{a,n}=n!^{-1}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\pi)U_\\pi$, using the\npermutation action and the orthogonal projections of\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections]]. Define\n$H_s^{(n)}=\\operatorname{ran}P_{s,n}$ and\n$H_a^{(n)}=\\operatorname{ran}P_{a,n}$, with\n$H_s^{(0)}=H_a^{(0)}=\\mathbb C$. These are the symmetric and antisymmetric\n$n$-particle sectors.\n\nThe bosonic and fermionic Fock Hilbert spaces are the square-summable direct\nsums\n$\\mathcal F_s(H)=\\{(\\Psi_n)_{n\\ge0}:\\Psi_n\\in H_s^{(n)},\\\n\\sum_n\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}$ and\n$\\mathcal F_a(H)=\\{(\\Psi_n)_{n\\ge0}:\\Psi_n\\in H_a^{(n)},\\\n\\sum_n\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}$, with the componentwise inner product.\nTheir vacuum is $\\Omega=(1,0,0,\\ldots)$. The finite-particle subspace\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ consists of sequences with only finitely many nonzero\ncomponents.\n\nFor $h\\in H$, the map $C_h:H\\to\\mathbb C$, $C_h(v)=\\langle h,v\\rangle_2$, is linear and bounded:\n$|C_h(v)|=|(v,h)_1|\\le\\|h\\|\\,\\|v\\|$ by\n[[thm-cauchy-schwarz-for-real-and-complex-inner-product-spaces]].\nFor any Hilbert space $K$, identify $\\mathbb C\\widehat\\otimes K$ with $K$\nby $J_K(z\\otimes v)=zv$. Every algebraic tensor is $1\\otimes w$;\nthe tensor norm is then $\\|w\\|$, so $J_K$ extends to a surjective isometry\non the completion (with inverse $w\\mapsto1\\otimes w$).\nThroughout the following formulas, $C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)}$ means\n$J_{H^{\\widehat\\otimes(n-1)}}\\circ(C_h\\widehat\\otimes I)$, with\n$I$ the identity on $H^{\\widehat\\otimes(n-1)}$.\nThe bounded tensor-map supplier therefore applies and gives norm at most\n$\\|h\\|$. This includes $n=1$, where $H^{\\widehat\\otimes0}=\\mathbb C$.\nContraction commutes with permutations of the remaining $n-1$ factors,\nso it takes symmetric or antisymmetric tensors to the corresponding\n$(n-1)$-particle sector (and to $\\mathbb C$ for $n=1$).\n\nFor $n\\ge0$, bosonic creation on sector $n$ is\n$a_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}P_{s,n+1}(h\\otimes\\Psi_n)$; for $n\\ge1$,\nbosonic annihilation is\n$a_n(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt n(C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)})\\Psi_n$, where\n$C_h$ is the bounded functional above, its tensor extension is supplied by\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-bounded-tensor-maps]], and $a(h)\\Omega=0$. Define fermionic creation\nby $c_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}P_{a,n+1}(h\\otimes\\Psi_n)$; for $n\\ge1$,\nfermionic annihilation is the corresponding first-factor contraction\n$c_n(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt n(C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)})\\Psi_n$, and\n$c(h)\\Omega=0$. The annihilation maps are conjugate-linear in $h$; the\ncreation maps are linear in $h$. These formulas define sector maps on the\nfinite-particle subspace; their maximal closed domains and operator relations are proved in\n[[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. No physical statistics assignment or spin–statistics\ntheorem is part of these mathematical definitions.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-composites-bounded-tensor-maps",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H,H',K,K'$ be complex Hilbert spaces and let $S\\in\\mathcal B(H,H')$, $T\\in\\mathcal B(K,K')$ be bounded linear operators ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]]). Write $H\\widehat\\otimes K$ for the completion of the algebraic tensor product constructed in [[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]], with inclusion $\\kappa$.\n\n(a) There is a unique bounded linear operator $S\\otimes T: H\\widehat\\otimes K\\to H'\\widehat\\otimes K'$ with $(S\\otimes T)\\kappa(u\\otimes v)=\\kappa'(Su\\otimes Tv)$ for all $u\\in H$, $v\\in K$, and it satisfies $\\|S\\otimes T\\|\\le\\|S\\|\\,\\|T\\|$ ([[def-operator-norm]]).\n\n(b) For Hilbert spaces $H'',K''$ and bounded $S':H''\\to H$, $T':K''\\to K$, one has $(S\\otimes T)(S'\\otimes T')=(SS')\\otimes(TT')$. In particular, for endomorphisms $S:H\\to H$, $T:K\\to K$, the endomorphisms $S\\otimes I_K$ and $I_H\\otimes T$ commute and their composite is $S\\otimes T$.\n\n(c) The Hilbert adjoint ([[def-hilbert-space-adjoint]]) is $(S\\otimes T)^*=S^*\\otimes T^*$.\n\n(d) If $S,T$ are unitary, then so is $S\\otimes T$. No extension of unbounded maps, and no algebraic manipulation of domains of unbounded operators, is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert space, $n\\ge1$, and $H^{\\otimes n}$ the $n$-fold completed tensor power ($H^{\\otimes1}:=H$, $H^{\\otimes(k+1)}:=H^{\\otimes k}\\widehat\\otimes H$ as in [[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]]). For $v_0,\\dots,v_{n-1}\\in H$ write $v_0\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_{n-1}\\in H^{\\otimes n}$ for the iterated elementary tensor.\n\n(a) For every $\\pi$ in the symmetric group $S_n$ ([[def-finite-symmetric-group-and-permutation-notation]]) there is a unique unitary $U_\\pi\\in\\mathcal B(H^{\\otimes n})$ with\n\n$$U_\\pi(v_0\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_{n-1})=v_{\\pi^{-1}(0)}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_{\\pi^{-1}(n-1)},$$\n\nand $U_\\pi U_\\sigma=U_{\\pi\\sigma}$, $U_{\\pi}^*=U_{\\pi^{-1}}$.\n\n(b) The operators\n\n$$P_+:=\\frac1{n!}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}U_\\pi,\\qquad P_-:=\\frac1{n!}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\pi)U_\\pi$$\n\n(with $\\operatorname{sgn}$ the sign homomorphism, [[thm-sign-is-a-homomorphism]]) are orthogonal projections.\n\n(c) $U_\\pi P_+=P_+U_\\pi=P_+$ and $U_\\pi P_-=P_-U_\\pi=\\operatorname{sgn}(\\pi)P_-$ for every $\\pi$; consequently the range of $P_+$ is the subspace of vectors fixed by every $U_\\pi$, and the range of $P_-$ is the subspace of vectors transforming by $\\operatorname{sgn}$.\n\n(d) If $v_0,\\dots,v_{n-1}$ have a repeated factor, that is, $v_i=v_j$ for some distinct $i,j\\in\\{0,\\dots,n-1\\}$, then $P_-(v_0\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_{n-1})=0$.\n\nSelecting the symmetric or antisymmetric sector for a particle species is a physical postulate, not a theorem of this construction; no spin-statistics statement is proved here.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Since $K_\\mu f,K_\\mu\\overline f\\in H_\\mu$ by [F9], the field formula is\ndefined on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. The first term $a(K_\\mu\\overline f)$ is\ncomplex-linear in $f$ because complex conjugation is followed by the\nconjugate-linear dependence of $a$; the second is linear because both\n$a^\\dagger$ and $K_\\mu$ are linear. Both terms preserve $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$\nby [F1,F2]. For real $f$, the shell vectors coincide, so\n$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f)\n=\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(K_\\mu f)|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}$. It is symmetric on the common\nfinite-particle domain by [F2]. Substituting $h=\\sqrt2K_\\mu f$ and $k=\\sqrt2K_\\mu g$ into [F3]'s\nWeyl relation gives the stated scalar phase, and its vacuum Gaussian gives\n$\\langle\\Omega,W_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2=e^{-\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2/2}$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "F9",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "For $g=(a,\\Lambda)$, [F10] gives the one-particle representation and\n$U_\\mu(g)K_\\mu f=K_\\mu f_g$. Since the affine action is real,\n$U_\\mu(g)K_\\mu\\overline f=K_\\mu\\overline{f_g}$. The Fock lift [F7]\nintertwines creation and annihilation, so the field formula in step 1.1 gives\n$$\\Gamma_s(U_\\mu(g))\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Gamma_s(U_\\mu(g))^{-1} =\\Phi_\\mu(f_g)$$\non $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. The explicit Segal Weyl formula [F3] and the same\nlift give bounded Weyl covariance. Strong continuity of the Fock lifts\nfollows from [F7] and [F10].",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F7",
            "F10",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "step": "2.2",
          "claim": "Since $a(K_\\mu\\overline f)\\Omega=0$ and\n$\\langle\\Omega,a^\\dagger(h)\\Psi\\rangle_2=0$, the vacuum one-point function\nvanishes. Expanding the two fields, only\n$a(K_\\mu\\overline f)a^\\dagger(K_\\mu g)\\Omega$ contributes to the vacuum\npairing; CCR [F2] makes that value\n$\\langle K_\\mu\\overline f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2$. Taking the first test to be $\\overline f$ and the second $f$ gives $\\|K_\\mu f\\|^2\\ge0$; for real $f$ this is the ordinary diagonal. For an ordered product of $n$ real smeared fields,\nwrite the first field as creation plus annihilation. The creation part\nvanishes against the left vacuum; commute the annihilation part successively\npast each later field using [F2]. Each commutator is the corresponding\ntwo-point pairing, and the remaining annihilator kills the right vacuum.\nThis yields the recursive sum over its possible partner and proves the\nWick pairing rule by induction on $n$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Cauchy–Schwarz gives $\\|C_h\\|\\le\\|h\\|$ for $C_h(v)=\\langle h,v\\rangle_2$. The tensor-extension bound and orthogonal symmetrizer in [F11] therefore give $\\|a_n(h)\\|\\le\\sqrt n\\|h\\|$ and $\\|a_n^\\dagger(h)\\|\\le\\sqrt{n+1}\\|h\\|$, the latter also using $\\|h\\otimes v\\|=\\|h\\|\\|v\\|$ from the product tensor pairing. Let $\\Theta$ have sectors only through $N$. These bounds and orthogonality of distinct output sectors imply $\\|\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Theta\\|\\le(\\sqrt N\\|K_\\mu\\overline f\\|+\\sqrt{N+1}\\|K_\\mu f\\|)\\|\\Theta\\|$. Continuity of $K_\\mu$ [F9] and conjugation [F12], followed by Hilbert Cauchy–Schwarz, makes every stated matrix coefficient continuous; complex linearity is step 1.1. Mutual adjoints [F2] give $\\langle\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Psi,\\Theta\\rangle_2=\\langle\\Psi,\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)\\Theta\\rangle_2$ for finite-particle vectors. Thus every such $\\Theta$ lies in the adjoint domain with the asserted restricted adjoint action. Finite-particle invariance permits reversing every finite polynomial product by this identity, so for $\\omega(B)=\\langle\\Omega,B\\Omega\\rangle_2$ one has $\\omega(B^*B)=\\|B\\Omega\\|^2\\ge0$ and $\\omega(I)=1$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F9",
            "F11",
            "F12",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-2",
          "step": "3.2",
          "claim": "For a Schwartz test $\\varphi(z)$, define $G_\\varphi(p)=\\int\\varphi(z)e^{-ip\\circ z}\\,d^4z$; for $F(x,y)\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^8)$ define $G_F(p)=\\int F(x,y)e^{-ip\\circ x}e^{ip\\circ y}\\,d^4x\\,d^4y$. By [F12], these are continuous Schwartz transforms, the second followed by the injective linear frequency restriction $L$ determined by the displayed phases. Injectivity in finite dimensions gives $|Lp|\\ge c|p|$ for some $c>0$; each derivative of a restricted transform is a fixed finite linear combination of its ambient derivatives at $Lp$. These bounds control every weighted restricted derivative by finitely many ambient Schwartz seminorms, proving the claimed restriction continuity and decay. In either case $|G(p)|\\le q_4(G)(1+|p|)^{-4}$, with $q_4$ a continuous Schwartz seminorm. The shell integral of $(1+|p|)^{-4}$ is finite by [F4]: its radial behavior is $O(r)$ at the massless apex, $O(r^2)$ near zero when $\\mu>0$, and $O(r^{-3})$ at infinity. Therefore the integrals of $G_\\varphi$ and $G_F$ against $d\\nu_\\mu$ define continuous complex-linear tempered distributions. On product tests $F(x,y)=f(x)g(y)$ the inner integral is $\\overline{K_\\mu\\overline f(p)}K_\\mu g(p)$; its shell integral is exactly the covariance from step 2.2. This proves the relative and bidistribution interfaces without assigning point operators.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F4",
            "F12",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "The one-particle translation phase is\n$e^{i(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)a^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf a)}$, so its real\ngenerator vector is $\\varpi(\\mathbf k)=(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),-\\mathbf k)$.\nIt is finite and measurable. The measure is sigma-finite by [F5], so [F8]\napplies and gives the joint PVM and vacuum at zero. On an $n$-particle\nsector the joint multiplier is $\\sum_{r=1}^n\n(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k_r),-\\mathbf k_r)$. Since\n$|\\sum_r\\mathbf k_r|\\le\\sum_r|\\mathbf k_r|\\le\\sum_r\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k_r)$,\nevery such value lies in the closed future cone; the vacuum value does too.\nThus the joint spectral measure is supported there, as claimed.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F8",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Smeared free scalar field and its Poincare representation: Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, let $H_\\mu$ and $K_\\mu$ be as in [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and form $\\mathcal F_s(H_\\mu)$ with vacuum $\\Omega$ and finite-particle domain $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ define on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ $$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu\\overline f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f).$$ This is a complex-linear map of test functions into operators $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\to\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For real $f$ it is symmetric and equals $\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(K_\\mu f)|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}$, where the Segal field of [[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]] is $\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(h)=\\overline{(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h))/\\sqrt2}$. Thus its Weyl unitary is $W_\\mu(f):= The specified witness, fixed coordinate space or supplied Hilbert/test objects are the inputs of this assertion; it does not assert a separate existence result after replacing them by an empty underlying set."
