        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Smeared free scalar field and its Poincare representation: Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, let $H_\\mu$ and $K_\\mu$ be as in [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and form $\\mathcal F_s(H_\\mu)$ with vacuum $\\Omega$ and finite-particle domain $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ define on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ $$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu\\overline f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f).$$ This is a complex-linear map of test functions into operators $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\to\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For real $f$ it is symmetric and equals $\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(K_\\mu f)|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}$, where the Segal field of [[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]] is $\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(h)=\\overline{(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h))/\\sqrt2}$. Thus its Weyl unitary is $W_\\mu(f):= The reverse iff axis adds no separately stated converse hypothesis here; the recorded claim is the displayed construction, identity, implication or scope qualification, not a characterization by a new necessary-and-sufficient condition."
