    "lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, let\n$H_\\mu$ and $K_\\mu$ be as in [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and form\n$\\mathcal F_s(H_\\mu)$ with vacuum $\\Omega$ and finite-particle domain\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ define on\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$\n$$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu\\overline f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f).$$\nThis is a complex-linear map of test functions into operators\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\to\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For real $f$ it is symmetric\nand equals $\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(K_\\mu f)|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}$, where the Segal field of\n[[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]] is\n$\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(h)=\\overline{(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h))/\\sqrt2}$.\nThus its Weyl unitary is\n$W_\\mu(f):=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2 K_\\mu f)$ and, for real $f,g$,\n$$W_\\mu(f)W_\\mu(g)= e^{-i\\,\\operatorname{Im}\\langle K_\\mu f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2}W_\\mu(f+g).$$\nFor every real $f$ the vacuum Weyl expectation is\n$$\\langle\\Omega,W_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =e^{-\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2/2}.$$\nHere the inner product is second-variable-linear as fixed in\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. The vacuum has zero one-point function and\ntwo-point function\n$$\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Phi_\\mu(g)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =\\langle K_\\mu\\overline f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2,$$\nwhich obeys $\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2=\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2\\ge0$ for every complex test $f$ (ordinary diagonal positivity holds for real tests); higher vacuum moments of ordered real smeared\nfields obey the CCR pairing recursion and hence the free-field Wick pairing\nrule. The field is a weak operator-valued tempered distribution on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$: every matrix coefficient $f\\mapsto\\langle\\Psi,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Theta\\rangle_2$ for $\\Psi,\\Theta\\in\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is a continuous complex-linear functional on Schwartz space. Its adjoint satisfies $\\Phi_\\mu(f)^*|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}=\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)$, and its normalized vacuum defines a positive state on the finite polynomial $*$-algebra generated by these fields.\n\nThe relative-coordinate two-point tempered distribution is\n$$W_\\mu(z)=\\int_{H_\\mu^+}e^{-ip\\circ z}\\,d\\nu_\\mu(p),$$\nunderstood by pairing with Schwartz tests. Its translation-invariant bidistribution is the shell integral of $e^{-ip\\circ x}e^{ip\\circ y}$; on product tests it is the displayed vacuum covariance.\n\nFor $g=(a,\\Lambda)$, let $f_g$ and $U_\\mu(g)$ be the affine test pullback\nand one-particle action of [[lem-qft-scalar-poincare-action]]. Their Fock\nlifts implement the same covariance for $\\Phi_\\mu$ on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$\nand for the bounded Weyl operators.\n\nFor translations, the joint spectral measure on Fock space is the\nsecond-quantized multiplier measure with one-particle vector\n$(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),-\\mathbf k)$. Its support is contained in the closed\nfuture cone $\\{(E,\\mathbf P):E\\ge|\\mathbf P|\\}$; the vacuum is an eigenvector\nat $(0,\\mathbf0)$. These are conditional mathematical model deductions.\nNo point field, empirical observation or measurement claim is made.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Here\n$\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ is the Schwartz space of smooth complex functions\nwhose coordinate-weighted derivatives are all bounded, as defined in\n[[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]].\nFix an oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski space and an inertial coordinate\nchart as in [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]. Use coordinates\n$x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$ and metric $\\eta=\\mathrm{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, matching the\n$(-+++)$ convention of the SR suppliers. Some QFT sources use its negative\n$(+---)$; the Lorentz group is unchanged, and the phase is fixed explicitly\nbelow.\nCoordinates $x$ have length units, momenta have inverse-length units, and the\nFourier phase below is dimensionless. A parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ has inverse-length\nunits and is only a mathematical shell parameter; it is not identified with a\nphysical rest mass. For $\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3$, set\n$$\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2},\\qquad p_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k).$$\nThe future mass shell is\n$$H_\\mu^+=\\{(p^0,\\mathbf p): (p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2=\\mu^2,\\ p^0>0\\}.$$\nWhen $\\mu=0$, its apex is omitted; thus $\\mathbf k\\ne0$ in the massless\nparameterization. The scalar shell pairing is\n$$p\\circ x:=p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x=-\\eta(p,x).$$\nDefine the measure and one-particle Hilbert space by\n$$d\\nu_\\mu(p_\\mu(\\mathbf k))= \\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)},\\qquad H_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu;\\mathbb C),$$\nwhere the omitted massless apex has measure zero. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb\nR^4)$ define its pointwise shell Fourier restriction by\n$$K_\\mu f(p)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}f(x)e^{ip\\circ x}\\,d^4x.$$\nThe Fourier convention is exactly the displayed integral, with no prefactor.\nIf $\\widehat f(\\xi^0,\\boldsymbol\\xi)=\n\\int f(x)e^{-2\\pi i(x^0\\xi^0+\\mathbf x\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\xi)}\\,d^4x$ denotes\nthe standard transform in\n[[cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space]],\nthen\n$$\\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p):=\\int f(x)e^{i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)}\\,d^4x =\\widehat f\\!\\left(-\\frac{p^0}{2\\pi},\\frac{\\mathbf p}{2\\pi}\\right).$$\nIts inverse is\n$$f(x)=(2\\pi)^{-4}\\int_{\\mathbb R^4} \\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p)e^{-i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)} \\,dp^0\\,d^3\\mathbf p.$$\nThus this convention is a time reflection and rescaling of the cited\nSchwartz automorphism and has the stated reciprocal normalization.\nThe proper orthochronous Lorentz group consists of linear maps preserving\n$\\eta$, orientation and the chosen future cone. A Poincare map is\n$x\\mapsto a+\\Lambda x$ with $a\\in\\mathbb R^4$ and $\\Lambda$ in that group.\n\nThis is a mathematical free scalar one-particle construction. It adopts the\nchosen coordinates, measure and Fourier convention; it asserts no experimental\nobservation, physical mass identification or measurement outcome.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] and let $H$ be a complex\nHilbert space with the second-variable-linear convention of\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. On $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ define the bosonic\ncreation and annihilation maps $a^\\dagger(h)$ and $a(h)$ and the fermionic maps\n$c^\\dagger(h)$ and $c(h)$ as in\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. Write $a_n(h)$ and $a_n^\\dagger(h)$ for\ntheir restrictions to the $n$th bosonic sector. For bosons set\n\n$$D(a(h))=\\{\\Psi\\in\\mathcal F_s(H):\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\|a_n(h)\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\},\\qquad D(a^\\dagger(h))=\\{\\Psi\\in\\mathcal F_s(H):\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\|a_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}.$$\n\nThen $\\mathcal F_s(H)$ and $\\mathcal F_a(H)$ are Hilbert spaces,\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is dense, and it is a graph core for both maximal\nbosonic sector maps. The bosonic operators on their displayed domains are\nclosed and mutually adjoint, and on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ satisfy\n\n$$[a(h),a^\\dagger(k)]=\\langle h,k\\rangle_2I,\\qquad [a(h),a(k)]=[a^\\dagger(h),a^\\dagger(k)]=0.$$\n\nThe fermionic $c(h)$ and $c^\\dagger(h)$ extend to bounded operators on the\nwhole Fock space, with norms $\\|h\\|$, and satisfy on the whole space\n\n$$\\{c(h),c^\\dagger(k)\\}=\\langle h,k\\rangle_2I,\\qquad \\{c(h),c(k)\\}=\\{c^\\dagger(h),c^\\dagger(k)\\}=0.$$\n\nThis is a mathematical construction of the two Fock representations; it does\nnot assign particle statistics to a physical species.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For an integer $d\\ge1$, let $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$ be the complex Schwartz space with seminorms\n$p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)=\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R^d}|x^\\alpha\\partial^\\beta f(x)|$\nfor all multi-indices $\\alpha,\\beta$; this is exactly\n[[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]]. Let\n$\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^d)=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ with the support convention of\n[[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]] and the locally convex LF topology of\n[[def-test-function-topology]]. The tempered-distribution space $\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^d)$\nis the space of continuous complex-linear functionals on this topological\n$\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$, paired bilinearly with test functions, as in\n[[def-tempered-distribution]].\n\nFor every complex Hilbert space $H$, use the second-variable-linear pairing\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_2:=(v,u)_1$, where $(\\cdot,\\cdot)_1$ is the first-variable-linear\nHilbert pairing of [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]. Thus\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_2$ is linear in $v$ and conjugate-linear in $u$.\nFor each tensor product, use the same norm completion as\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]], but equip it with the\nsecond-variable-linear pairing obtained by conjugating that supplier's\nfirst-variable-linear pairing. This leaves the norm and completion unchanged;\non elementary tensors it gives the product of the second-variable pairings.\nLet $H^{\\widehat\\otimes n}$ be the iterated Hilbert tensor completion, with\n$H^{\\widehat\\otimes0}:=\\mathbb C$; the tensor inner product is the product of\nthe factor pairings. Identify parenthesizations by the isometry fixing\nelementary tensors: the product pairing is the same on their dense span,\nso the map and its inverse extend to the completions. For $n\\ge1$, let\n$P_{s,n}=n!^{-1}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}U_\\pi$ and\n$P_{a,n}=n!^{-1}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\pi)U_\\pi$, using the\npermutation action and the orthogonal projections of\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections]]. Define\n$H_s^{(n)}=\\operatorname{ran}P_{s,n}$ and\n$H_a^{(n)}=\\operatorname{ran}P_{a,n}$, with\n$H_s^{(0)}=H_a^{(0)}=\\mathbb C$. These are the symmetric and antisymmetric\n$n$-particle sectors.\n\nThe bosonic and fermionic Fock Hilbert spaces are the square-summable direct\nsums\n$\\mathcal F_s(H)=\\{(\\Psi_n)_{n\\ge0}:\\Psi_n\\in H_s^{(n)},\\\n\\sum_n\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}$ and\n$\\mathcal F_a(H)=\\{(\\Psi_n)_{n\\ge0}:\\Psi_n\\in H_a^{(n)},\\\n\\sum_n\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}$, with the componentwise inner product.\nTheir vacuum is $\\Omega=(1,0,0,\\ldots)$. The finite-particle subspace\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ consists of sequences with only finitely many nonzero\ncomponents.\n\nFor $h\\in H$, the map $C_h:H\\to\\mathbb C$, $C_h(v)=\\langle h,v\\rangle_2$, is linear and bounded:\n$|C_h(v)|=|(v,h)_1|\\le\\|h\\|\\,\\|v\\|$ by\n[[thm-cauchy-schwarz-for-real-and-complex-inner-product-spaces]].\nFor any Hilbert space $K$, identify $\\mathbb C\\widehat\\otimes K$ with $K$\nby $J_K(z\\otimes v)=zv$. Every algebraic tensor is $1\\otimes w$;\nthe tensor norm is then $\\|w\\|$, so $J_K$ extends to a surjective isometry\non the completion (with inverse $w\\mapsto1\\otimes w$).\nThroughout the following formulas, $C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)}$ means\n$J_{H^{\\widehat\\otimes(n-1)}}\\circ(C_h\\widehat\\otimes I)$, with\n$I$ the identity on $H^{\\widehat\\otimes(n-1)}$.\nThe bounded tensor-map supplier therefore applies and gives norm at most\n$\\|h\\|$. This includes $n=1$, where $H^{\\widehat\\otimes0}=\\mathbb C$.\nContraction commutes with permutations of the remaining $n-1$ factors,\nso it takes symmetric or antisymmetric tensors to the corresponding\n$(n-1)$-particle sector (and to $\\mathbb C$ for $n=1$).\n\nFor $n\\ge0$, bosonic creation on sector $n$ is\n$a_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}P_{s,n+1}(h\\otimes\\Psi_n)$; for $n\\ge1$,\nbosonic annihilation is\n$a_n(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt n(C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)})\\Psi_n$, where\n$C_h$ is the bounded functional above, its tensor extension is supplied by\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-bounded-tensor-maps]], and $a(h)\\Omega=0$. Define fermionic creation\nby $c_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}P_{a,n+1}(h\\otimes\\Psi_n)$; for $n\\ge1$,\nfermionic annihilation is the corresponding first-factor contraction\n$c_n(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt n(C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)})\\Psi_n$, and\n$c(h)\\Omega=0$. The annihilation maps are conjugate-linear in $h$; the\ncreation maps are linear in $h$. These formulas define sector maps on the\nfinite-particle subspace; their maximal closed domains and operator relations are proved in\n[[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. No physical statistics assignment or spin–statistics\ntheorem is part of these mathematical definitions.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-fourier-inversion-on-schwartz-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. For every $f\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^n)$ and every $x\\in\\mathbb R^n$,\n$$f(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}\\widehat f(\\xi)e^{2\\pi ix\\cdot\\xi}\\,d\\xi.$$\nThe integral is absolutely convergent.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-tempered-distribution",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix an integer $n\\geq1$.  A **tempered distribution** on $\\mathbb R^n$ is a\ncontinuous complex-linear functional\n\n$$u:\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^n)\\longrightarrow\\mathbb C,$$\n\nwhere Schwartz space and its locally convex topology are those of\n[[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]] and\n[[def-schwartz-topology-and-convergence]].  The space of all such functionals\nis denoted $\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^n)$.\n\nWe write $\\langle u,\\varphi\\rangle=u(\\varphi)$.  This pairing is bilinear:\nthe test variable is not conjugated.  Thus $u(a\\varphi+b\\psi)=\na\\langle u,\\varphi\\rangle+b\\langle u,\\psi\\rangle$ for $a,b\\in\\mathbb C$.\nThe zero functional is tempered.  Continuity is topological continuity, and,\nbecause the Schwartz topology is generated by a countable cofinal family of\nseminorms, it is equivalently continuity on every convergent sequence.  No\nchoice principle is used in this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "An **oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space** is a quadruple\n$(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ with the following data. In this notation $V$ includes its\nspecified orientation; forgetting that orientation gives only the underlying\nreal vector space, not the full datum $V$ of the model.\n\n1. A nonempty affine space $A$ over a $4$-dimensional real vector space $V$,\n   with action $(p,v)\\mapsto p+v$ and displacement $q-p\\in V$\n   ([[def-affine-space-and-displacement]]). In particular $V$ is a real vector\n   space of dimension $4$.\n2. A Lorentz form $\\eta$ on $V$, i.e. a nondegenerate symmetric bilinear form of\n   inertia $(3,1,0)$ ([[def-lorentz-form-and-inertia]]). Its associated quadratic\n   form is $q(v)=\\eta(v,v)$.\n3. An **orientation** of $V$: a positive ray of the determinant line\n   $\\Lambda^4V$, fixed once and for all\n   ([[def-determinant-line-orientation-of-a-finite-dimensional-real-vector-space]]).\n4. A **time orientation** $C^+$ of $(V,\\eta)$: one of the two connected\n   components of the timelike set $T=\\{v\\in V:\\eta(v,v)<0\\}$, the other being\n   $C^-:=T\\setminus C^+$ ([[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]]).\n\nThe following terminology is attached to such a quadruple.\n\n- The elements $p$ of $A$ are the **events**; for events $p,q$ the vector\n  $q-p\\in V$ is the **displacement from $p$ to $q$**.\n- The **interval** of $p,q\\in A$ is the real number\n  $I(p,q):=\\eta(q-p,q-p)=q(q-p)$.\n- Events $p,q$ are **timelike**, **null**, **spacelike** or **causally\n  related** when the displacement $q-p$ is timelike, null, spacelike or causal\n  in the sense of [[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]]; they are\n  **future-related** when $q-p$ is future causal and **past-related** when\n  $q-p$ is past causal in the sense of\n  [[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]].\n\n**No point of $A$ is distinguished.** For every $v\\in V$ the translation\n$\\tau_v:A\\to A$, $\\tau_v(p):=p+v$, is a bijection, since it is inverse to\n$\\tau_{-v}$: $\\tau_{-v}(\\tau_v(p))=(p+v)+(-v)=p+(v-v)=p$ and symmetrically, by\nthe affine action axioms ([[def-affine-space-and-displacement]]). Displacements\nare translation invariant, $\\tau_v(q)-\\tau_v(p)=q-p$, so every $\\tau_v$ preserves\nthe interval, the causal classification of every ordered pair, and the sets\n$C^+$, $C^-$ (which are subsets of $V$, independent of the point of $A$); it\nchanges only the labelling of points. The mathematical structure of the model is\nexactly the quadruple $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$; no derived physical quantity (proper\ntime, four-velocity, momentum, energy, charge or any field) is part of this\ndefinition, and all later structure of the framework (frames, charts, tensor\nfields, worldlines) is built on this model.\n\n**Well-definedness.** The affine-space definition supplies the action and the\nunique displacements; the Lorentz-form definition supplies $\\eta$ and its\ninertia; the determinant-line definition supplies orientations; and the two\ncomponents of $T$ between which the time-orientation definition chooses exist by\nthe components lemma recorded in the well-definedness clause of\n[[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]], which is also what makes the\nnames future and past causal in item 4 well defined.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $0<r<R$ and $n\\ge 1$. Then there exists a smooth function $\\rho:\\mathbb R^n\\to [0,1]$ such that $\\rho=1$ on $\\overline B_r(0)$ and $\\operatorname{supp}(\\rho)\\subseteq B_R(0)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ],
          "reviewed": {
            "upheld": true,
            "by": "original QFT prerequisite owner /root/resolve_pending_inputs_5_60",
            "reason": "The actual Statement and Proof use a spatial mollifier on R^3. Apply the supplier with n=3 and positive concentric radii (for example1/2 and1), then translate/rescale and divide by its positive compact integral. No n=0 or unrestricted dimension theorem is asserted by this fixed spatial-use clause."
          }
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Here\n$\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ is the Schwartz space of smooth complex functions\nwhose coordinate-weighted derivatives are all bounded, as defined in\n[[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]].\nFix an oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski space and an inertial coordinate\nchart as in [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]. Use coordinates\n$x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$ and metric $\\eta=\\mathrm{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, matching the\n$(-+++)$ convention of the SR suppliers. Some QFT sources use its negative\n$(+---)$; the Lorentz group is unchanged, and the phase is fixed explicitly\nbelow.\nCoordinates $x$ have length units, momenta have inverse-length units, and the\nFourier phase below is dimensionless. A parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ has inverse-length\nunits and is only a mathematical shell parameter; it is not identified with a\nphysical rest mass. For $\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3$, set\n$$\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2},\\qquad p_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k).$$\nThe future mass shell is\n$$H_\\mu^+=\\{(p^0,\\mathbf p): (p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2=\\mu^2,\\ p^0>0\\}.$$\nWhen $\\mu=0$, its apex is omitted; thus $\\mathbf k\\ne0$ in the massless\nparameterization. The scalar shell pairing is\n$$p\\circ x:=p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x=-\\eta(p,x).$$\nDefine the measure and one-particle Hilbert space by\n$$d\\nu_\\mu(p_\\mu(\\mathbf k))= \\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)},\\qquad H_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu;\\mathbb C),$$\nwhere the omitted massless apex has measure zero. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb\nR^4)$ define its pointwise shell Fourier restriction by\n$$K_\\mu f(p)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}f(x)e^{ip\\circ x}\\,d^4x.$$\nThe Fourier convention is exactly the displayed integral, with no prefactor.\nIf $\\widehat f(\\xi^0,\\boldsymbol\\xi)=\n\\int f(x)e^{-2\\pi i(x^0\\xi^0+\\mathbf x\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\xi)}\\,d^4x$ denotes\nthe standard transform in\n[[cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space]],\nthen\n$$\\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p):=\\int f(x)e^{i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)}\\,d^4x =\\widehat f\\!\\left(-\\frac{p^0}{2\\pi},\\frac{\\mathbf p}{2\\pi}\\right).$$\nIts inverse is\n$$f(x)=(2\\pi)^{-4}\\int_{\\mathbb R^4} \\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p)e^{-i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)} \\,dp^0\\,d^3\\mathbf p.$$\nThus this convention is a time reflection and rescaling of the cited\nSchwartz automorphism and has the stated reciprocal normalization.\nThe proper orthochronous Lorentz group consists of linear maps preserving\n$\\eta$, orientation and the chosen future cone. A Poincare map is\n$x\\mapsto a+\\Lambda x$ with $a\\in\\mathbb R^4$ and $\\Lambda$ in that group.\n\nThis is a mathematical free scalar one-particle construction. It adopts the\nchosen coordinates, measure and Fourier convention; it asserts no experimental\nobservation, physical mass identification or measurement outcome.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. The Fourier transform is a topological automorphism of $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^n)$, with $\\mathcal F^2=R$ and $\\mathcal F^{-1}=R\\mathcal F$, where $Rf(x)=f(-x)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix an integer $n\\ge1$. With the complex smooth-function convention of [[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]] and multi-indices of [[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]], define\n$$p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)=\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R^n}|x^\\alpha\\partial^\\beta f(x)|,\\qquad \\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^n)=\\{f\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C):p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)<\\infty\\text{ for all }\\alpha,\\beta\\}.$$\nThe zero multi-index gives $x^0=1$ and $\\partial^0f=f$. These are actual smooth functions, not equivalence classes. The notation $C_c^\\infty$ uses the support convention in [[def-c-c-and-c-c-infinity-on-rn]].\n\nPointwise differentiation is linear; thus $p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f+g)\\le p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)+p_{\\alpha\\beta}(g)$ and $p_{\\alpha\\beta}(cf)=|c|p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)$. In particular this set is a complex vector space and each $p_{\\alpha\\beta}$ is a seminorm. The zero function belongs to it. Since $p_{00}(f)=0$ forces $f(x)=0$ for every $x$, the family separates functions. No choice principle is needed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qft-mass-shell-test-range-dense",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. For every $\\mu\\ge0$, the\nspace $H_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu)$ of\n[[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]] is separable (has a countable norm-dense subset), and\n$$\\{K_\\mu f:f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)\\}$$\nis dense in $H_\\mu$. If $\\mu>0$, the range contains every function in\n$C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$; if $\\mu=0$, it contains every function in\n$C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\})$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "claim": "Assume Countable Choice as required by [F4]. For a spacetime test\n$\\varphi\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)$ set\n$q_\\varphi(t,\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\varphi(t,\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x$.\nIntegration by parts in the spatial variables gives\n$$(1+|\\mathbf k|^2)^2q_\\varphi(t,\\mathbf k)=\\int e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}(1-\\Delta_{\\mathbf x})^2\\varphi(t,\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x.$$\nLet $P(\\varphi)$ be the maximum, over spatial multi-indices $|\\beta|\\le4$,\nof $\\sup_{t,\\mathbf x}(1+|t|)^4(1+|\\mathbf x|^2)^2|\\partial_{\\mathbf x}^\\beta\\varphi(t,\\mathbf x)|$.\nBy [F9] this is bounded by a finite sum of the defining Schwartz seminorms.\nSince $\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}(1+|\\mathbf x|^2)^{-2}\\,d^3\\mathbf x<\\infty$,\n$$|q_\\varphi(t,\\mathbf k)|\\le C P(\\varphi)(1+|t|)^{-4}(1+|\\mathbf k|^2)^{-2}.$$\nFor every $\\mu\\ge0$, $|\\sin(\\omega_\\mu t)/\\omega_\\mu|\\le|t|$ off the\nmassless apex. Consequently the joint pairing\n$$\\langle\\Delta_\\mu,\\varphi\\rangle=(2\\pi)^{-3}\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{\\sin(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)t)}{\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)}q_\\varphi(t,\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k\\,dt$$\nis absolutely convergent and has modulus at most $C'P(\\varphi)$, because\n$\\int |t|(1+|t|)^{-4}\\,dt$ and $\\int(1+|\\mathbf k|^2)^{-2}\\,d^3\\mathbf k$\nare finite. It is complex-linear and continuous on $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)$,\nso it defines the claimed spacetime tempered distribution [F5]. The estimate\nalso supplies the required polynomial growth in time and proves that this\npairing is the integral of its fixed-time spatial pairings. The apex value\ncan be assigned arbitrarily without changing any integral; the separate\n$1/|\\mathbf k|$ shell factor is locally integrable in three dimensions.\nFor the fixed-time family, derivatives of $\\sin(\\omega_\\mu t)/\\omega_\\mu$\nhave at most polynomial growth in $\\mathbf k$, uniformly on bounded time\nintervals. Taking enough spatial Schwartz weights therefore permits all time\nderivatives under its spatial pairing. It follows both for this family and,\nby integration by parts in $t$, for the joint distribution that\n$(\\partial_t^2-\\Delta_{\\mathbf x}+\\mu^2)\\Delta_\\mu=0$,\n$\\Delta_\\mu(0,\\cdot)=0$, and\n$\\partial_t\\Delta_\\mu(0,\\cdot)=\\delta_0$; the last identity is Fourier\ninversion [F4].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F9",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "claim": "Choose a nonnegative smooth spatial mollifier $\\rho_\\epsilon$ of integral\none supported in $|\\mathbf x|<\\epsilon$, using [F7], and let\n$u_\\epsilon(t,\\cdot)=\\Delta_\\mu(t,\\cdot)*\\rho_\\epsilon$. Its Fourier\nmultiplier is $\\widehat\\rho_\\epsilon(\\mathbf k)\\sin(\\omega_\\mu t)/\\omega_\\mu$;\nthis is Schwartz in $\\mathbf k$ for every fixed $t$, including $\\mu=0$\nbecause $\\sin(|\\mathbf k|t)/|\\mathbf k|$ has a power series in\n$|\\mathbf k|^2$. Thus $u_\\epsilon$ and its derivatives are Schwartz and have\nfinite energy by [F8]. By 1.1 it is a smooth solution of the\nKlein–Gordon equation with data\n$u_\\epsilon(0)=0$, $\\partial_tu_\\epsilon(0)=\\rho_\\epsilon$. Its initial\nenergy is finite. Choose $R>\\epsilon$ and for $t\\ge0$ set\n$$E_R(t)=\\frac12\\int_{|\\mathbf x|>R+t} \\bigl(|\\partial_tu_\\epsilon|^2+|\\nabla u_\\epsilon|^2+ \\mu^2|u_\\epsilon|^2\\bigr)\\,d^3\\mathbf x.$$\nThe equation and integration by parts on the exterior region give\n$$E_R'(t)=-\\frac12\\int_{|\\mathbf x|=R+t} \\left(|\\partial_tu_\\epsilon+\\partial_ru_\\epsilon|^2+ |\\nabla_Tu_\\epsilon|^2+\\mu^2|u_\\epsilon|^2\\right)dS\\le0.$$\nFor a complex solution apply this identity to real and imaginary parts.\nCutoff at a large radius and use finite energy to pass to the exterior\nintegral. Since $E_R(0)=0$ and $E_R(t)\\ge0$, it vanishes for all $t\\ge0$.\nFor $\\mu>0$ this forces $u_\\epsilon=0$ outside $R+t$; for $\\mu=0$ it forces\nits spatial gradient and time derivative to vanish there, so it is constant\non the connected exterior and the inverse Fourier transform tends to zero at\nspatial infinity, hence the constant is zero. Reversing time gives support\ninside $|\\mathbf x|\\le R+|t|$ for both signs of $t$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-3-1",
          "claim": "Let $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$ and choose $R=2\\epsilon$. For every compact smooth test $\\varphi(t,\\mathbf x)$, its spatial convolution with the reflected mollifier converges with all derivatives uniformly on one compact set to $\\varphi$; this follows by writing the difference as the average of $\\varphi(t,\\mathbf x-\\mathbf y)-\\varphi(t,\\mathbf x)$ for $|\\mathbf y|<\\epsilon$ and applying the mean-value bound to each derivative. Continuity of the tempered distribution [F5] therefore gives $\\Delta_\\mu*_{\\,\\mathbf x}\\rho_\\epsilon\\to\\Delta_\\mu$ distributionally. If a compact test support lies in the spacelike open set $|\\mathbf x|>|t|$, its positive distance from the closed cone on that compact set gives a margin $\\delta>0$; choosing $3\\epsilon<\\delta$ makes its pairing with $u_\\epsilon$ vanish by 2.1. Passing to the distributional limit proves the support inclusion.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-4-1",
          "claim": "Under [A1], [F3] supplies separability of $H_\\mu$ for every $\\mu\\ge0$, so the CCR lemma applies to the shell Fock space. For real $f,g$, expand the commutator using [F1]. The two like-type\ncommutators vanish; [F3] leaves\n$$\\langle K_\\mu\\overline f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2 -\\langle K_\\mu\\overline g,K_\\mu f\\rangle_2.$$\nDefine the compact smooth difference test\n$h(z)=\\int f(z+y)g(y)\\,d^4y$. Thus the double smearing in the Statement\nmeans $\\langle\\Delta_\\mu,h\\rangle$, which is well-defined by 1.1.\nSubstitute the shell integrals [F2], use the second-variable-linear pairing,\nand combine the two exponentials. The Fourier transform of $h$ is the\nproduct of the corresponding transforms of $f$ and $g$, by ordinary Fubini\nfor compact tests. The resulting shell integrals are absolutely convergent:\nthe transforms decay faster than any power and the massless $1/|\\mathbf k|$\nfactor is locally integrable. This justifies the comparison with the joint\npairing in 1.1, without interchanging an unsmeared oscillatory integral. The result is\n$-i\\int f(x)g(y)\\Delta_\\mu(x-y)\\,d^4x\\,d^4y$; the sign follows from\n$e^{-ip\\circ(x-y)}-e^{ip\\circ(x-y)}=-2i\\sin(p\\circ(x-y))$ and the\ndefinition of $\\Delta_\\mu$. If every pair $(x,y)$ in the two supports is\nspacelike separated, then $x-y$ lies outside the support in 3.1, so the\ndistributional pairing vanishes.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Causal support of the scalar commutator distribution: Assume Countable Choice. For $\\mu\\ge0$, define the tempered distribution $$\\Delta_\\mu(t,\\mathbf x)= \\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3} \\frac{\\sin(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)t)}{\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)} e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x},$$ with the massless integrand defined off $\\mathbf k=0$; its $1/|\\mathbf k|$ behavior is locally integrable in three dimensions. Then $$\\operatorname{supp}\\Delta_\\mu \\subseteq\\{(t,\\mathbf x):t^2-|\\mathbf x|^2\\ge0\\},$$ the closed causal cone for the $(-+++)$ metric. For real compactly supported smooth $f,g$, put $$\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)= \\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R^4} f(x)g(y)\\Delta_\\mu(x-y)\\,d^4x\\,d^4y.$$ On $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ the smeared fields of [[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]] satisfy $$[\\Phi_\\mu(f),\\Phi_\\mu(g)]=-i\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)I.$$ Consequently spacelike-separated supports give commuting fields. The specified witness, fixed coordinate space or supplied Hilbert/test objects are the inputs of this assertion; it does not assert a separate existence result after replacing them by an empty underlying set."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 gives Delta(0)=0 and the initial derivative at t=0."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Causal support of the scalar commutator distribution: Assume Countable Choice. For $\\mu\\ge0$, define the tempered distribution $$\\Delta_\\mu(t,\\mathbf x)= \\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3} \\frac{\\sin(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)t)}{\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)} e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x},$$ with the massless integrand defined off $\\mathbf k=0$; its $1/|\\mathbf k|$ behavior is locally integrable in three dimensions. Then $$\\operatorname{supp}\\Delta_\\mu \\subseteq\\{(t,\\mathbf x):t^2-|\\mathbf x|^2\\ge0\\},$$ the closed causal cone for the $(-+++)$ metric. For real compactly supported smooth $f,g$, put $$\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)= \\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R^4} f(x)g(y)\\Delta_\\mu(x-y)\\,d^4x\\,d^4y.$$ On $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ the smeared fields of [[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]] satisfy $$[\\Phi_\\mu(f),\\Phi_\\mu(g)]=-i\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)I.$$ Consequently spacelike-separated supports give commuting fields. The objects have the fixed fiber, test domain, witness or shell specified there. This claim does not vary a finite set cardinality through a singleton boundary; any displayed unit scalar/vector is part of the stated data."
        },
