        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Causal support of the scalar commutator distribution: Assume Countable Choice. For $\\mu\\ge0$, define the tempered distribution $$\\Delta_\\mu(t,\\mathbf x)= \\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3} \\frac{\\sin(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)t)}{\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)} e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x},$$ with the massless integrand defined off $\\mathbf k=0$; its $1/|\\mathbf k|$ behavior is locally integrable in three dimensions. Then $$\\operatorname{supp}\\Delta_\\mu \\subseteq\\{(t,\\mathbf x):t^2-|\\mathbf x|^2\\ge0\\},$$ the closed causal cone for the $(-+++)$ metric. For real compactly supported smooth $f,g$, put $$\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)= \\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R^4} f(x)g(y)\\Delta_\\mu(x-y)\\,d^4x\\,d^4y.$$ On $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ the smeared fields of [[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]] satisfy $$[\\Phi_\\mu(f),\\Phi_\\mu(g)]=-i\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)I.$$ Consequently spacelike-separated supports give commuting fields. The forward iff axis adds no separately stated converse hypothesis here; the recorded claim is the displayed construction, identity, implication or scope qualification, not a characterization by a new necessary-and-sufficient condition."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Causal support of the scalar commutator distribution: Assume Countable Choice. For $\\mu\\ge0$, define the tempered distribution $$\\Delta_\\mu(t,\\mathbf x)= \\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3} \\frac{\\sin(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)t)}{\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)} e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x},$$ with the massless integrand defined off $\\mathbf k=0$; its $1/|\\mathbf k|$ behavior is locally integrable in three dimensions. Then $$\\operatorname{supp}\\Delta_\\mu \\subseteq\\{(t,\\mathbf x):t^2-|\\mathbf x|^2\\ge0\\},$$ the closed causal cone for the $(-+++)$ metric. For real compactly supported smooth $f,g$, put $$\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)= \\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R^4} f(x)g(y)\\Delta_\\mu(x-y)\\,d^4x\\,d^4y.$$ On $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ the smeared fields of [[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]] satisfy $$[\\Phi_\\mu(f),\\Phi_\\mu(g)]=-i\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)I.$$ Consequently spacelike-separated supports give commuting fields. The reverse iff axis adds no separately stated converse hypothesis here; the recorded claim is the displayed construction, identity, implication or scope qualification, not a characterization by a new necessary-and-sufficient condition."
        }
