      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "alpha-batch-38",
        "notes": "Checked four proof steps and all eleven direct suppliers. Joint spacetime pairing is bounded by a finite Schwartz-seminorm sum using |sin(omega t)/omega|<=|t|; spatial/time integrals of the stated majorants are finite, so temperedness is genuine including mu=0. Fourier inversion gives initial data (0,delta_0) and KG evolution. Mollification produces a smooth Schwartz solution; exterior energy differentiation has the correct outward-moving-boundary sign and gives -(ut+ur)^2, tangential gradient and mu^2 terms. Zero initial exterior energy forces vanishing; at mu=0 constant exterior values vanish by Schwartz decay. Time reversal and R=2epsilon establish causal support after compact-test mollifier convergence. Compact difference test h(z)=integral f(z+y)g(y) gives legitimate double smearing; decaying shell transforms justify CCR comparison. The phase difference has sign -i Delta. Pairwise spacelike support ensures h is supported in the spacelike complement; zero tests and either time direction are included. Tong 2.163-2.167 and Fewster-Rejzner 4.2 support the formal kernel/locality conventions; full energy and distributional argument is local. Accept paragraph/layout changes; no mathematical defect confirmed."
      }
    },
    "lem-qft-scalar-local-weyl-algebra": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, let\n$H_\\mu$ and $K_\\mu$ be as in [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and form\n$\\mathcal F_s(H_\\mu)$ with vacuum $\\Omega$ and finite-particle domain\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ define on\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$\n$$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu\\overline f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f).$$\nThis is a complex-linear map of test functions into operators\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\to\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For real $f$ it is symmetric\nand equals $\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(K_\\mu f)|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}$, where the Segal field of\n[[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]] is\n$\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(h)=\\overline{(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h))/\\sqrt2}$.\nThus its Weyl unitary is\n$W_\\mu(f):=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2 K_\\mu f)$ and, for real $f,g$,\n$$W_\\mu(f)W_\\mu(g)= e^{-i\\,\\operatorname{Im}\\langle K_\\mu f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2}W_\\mu(f+g).$$\nFor every real $f$ the vacuum Weyl expectation is\n$$\\langle\\Omega,W_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =e^{-\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2/2}.$$\nHere the inner product is second-variable-linear as fixed in\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. The vacuum has zero one-point function and\ntwo-point function\n$$\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Phi_\\mu(g)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =\\langle K_\\mu\\overline f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2,$$\nwhich obeys $\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2=\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2\\ge0$ for every complex test $f$ (ordinary diagonal positivity holds for real tests); higher vacuum moments of ordered real smeared\nfields obey the CCR pairing recursion and hence the free-field Wick pairing\nrule. The field is a weak operator-valued tempered distribution on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$: every matrix coefficient $f\\mapsto\\langle\\Psi,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Theta\\rangle_2$ for $\\Psi,\\Theta\\in\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is a continuous complex-linear functional on Schwartz space. Its adjoint satisfies $\\Phi_\\mu(f)^*|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}=\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)$, and its normalized vacuum defines a positive state on the finite polynomial $*$-algebra generated by these fields.\n\nThe relative-coordinate two-point tempered distribution is\n$$W_\\mu(z)=\\int_{H_\\mu^+}e^{-ip\\circ z}\\,d\\nu_\\mu(p),$$\nunderstood by pairing with Schwartz tests. Its translation-invariant bidistribution is the shell integral of $e^{-ip\\circ x}e^{ip\\circ y}$; on product tests it is the displayed vacuum covariance.\n\nFor $g=(a,\\Lambda)$, let $f_g$ and $U_\\mu(g)$ be the affine test pullback\nand one-particle action of [[lem-qft-scalar-poincare-action]]. Their Fock\nlifts implement the same covariance for $\\Phi_\\mu$ on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$\nand for the bounded Weyl operators.\n\nFor translations, the joint spectral measure on Fock space is the\nsecond-quantized multiplier measure with one-particle vector\n$(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),-\\mathbf k)$. Its support is contained in the closed\nfuture cone $\\{(E,\\mathbf P):E\\ge|\\mathbf P|\\}$; the vacuum is an eigenvector\nat $(0,\\mathbf0)$. These are conditional mathematical model deductions.\nNo point field, empirical observation or measurement claim is made.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. For $\\mu\\ge0$, define the tempered distribution\n$$\\Delta_\\mu(t,\\mathbf x)= \\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3} \\frac{\\sin(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)t)}{\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)} e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x},$$\nwith the massless integrand defined off $\\mathbf k=0$; its $1/|\\mathbf k|$\nbehavior is locally integrable in three dimensions. Then\n$$\\operatorname{supp}\\Delta_\\mu \\subseteq\\{(t,\\mathbf x):t^2-|\\mathbf x|^2\\ge0\\},$$\nthe closed causal cone for the $(-+++)$ metric. For real compactly supported\nsmooth $f,g$, put\n$$\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)= \\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R^4} f(x)g(y)\\Delta_\\mu(x-y)\\,d^4x\\,d^4y.$$\nOn $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ the smeared fields of\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]] satisfy\n$$[\\Phi_\\mu(f),\\Phi_\\mu(g)]=-i\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)I.$$\nConsequently spacelike-separated supports give commuting fields.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, let\n$H_\\mu$ and $K_\\mu$ be as in [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and form\n$\\mathcal F_s(H_\\mu)$ with vacuum $\\Omega$ and finite-particle domain\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ define on\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$\n$$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu\\overline f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f).$$\nThis is a complex-linear map of test functions into operators\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\to\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For real $f$ it is symmetric\nand equals $\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(K_\\mu f)|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}$, where the Segal field of\n[[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]] is\n$\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(h)=\\overline{(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h))/\\sqrt2}$.\nThus its Weyl unitary is\n$W_\\mu(f):=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2 K_\\mu f)$ and, for real $f,g$,\n$$W_\\mu(f)W_\\mu(g)= e^{-i\\,\\operatorname{Im}\\langle K_\\mu f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2}W_\\mu(f+g).$$\nFor every real $f$ the vacuum Weyl expectation is\n$$\\langle\\Omega,W_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =e^{-\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2/2}.$$\nHere the inner product is second-variable-linear as fixed in\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. The vacuum has zero one-point function and\ntwo-point function\n$$\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Phi_\\mu(g)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =\\langle K_\\mu\\overline f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2,$$\nwhich obeys $\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2=\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2\\ge0$ for every complex test $f$ (ordinary diagonal positivity holds for real tests); higher vacuum moments of ordered real smeared\nfields obey the CCR pairing recursion and hence the free-field Wick pairing\nrule. The field is a weak operator-valued tempered distribution on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$: every matrix coefficient $f\\mapsto\\langle\\Psi,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Theta\\rangle_2$ for $\\Psi,\\Theta\\in\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is a continuous complex-linear functional on Schwartz space. Its adjoint satisfies $\\Phi_\\mu(f)^*|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}=\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)$, and its normalized vacuum defines a positive state on the finite polynomial $*$-algebra generated by these fields.\n\nThe relative-coordinate two-point tempered distribution is\n$$W_\\mu(z)=\\int_{H_\\mu^+}e^{-ip\\circ z}\\,d\\nu_\\mu(p),$$\nunderstood by pairing with Schwartz tests. Its translation-invariant bidistribution is the shell integral of $e^{-ip\\circ x}e^{ip\\circ y}$; on product tests it is the displayed vacuum covariance.\n\nFor $g=(a,\\Lambda)$, let $f_g$ and $U_\\mu(g)$ be the affine test pullback\nand one-particle action of [[lem-qft-scalar-poincare-action]]. Their Fock\nlifts implement the same covariance for $\\Phi_\\mu$ on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$\nand for the bounded Weyl operators.\n\nFor translations, the joint spectral measure on Fock space is the\nsecond-quantized multiplier measure with one-particle vector\n$(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),-\\mathbf k)$. Its support is contained in the closed\nfuture cone $\\{(E,\\mathbf P):E\\ge|\\mathbf P|\\}$; the vacuum is an eigenvector\nat $(0,\\mathbf0)$. These are conditional mathematical model deductions.\nNo point field, empirical observation or measurement claim is made.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] and let $H$ be a separable\ncomplex Hilbert space, with separability taken in its Hilbert norm topology\nas defined in [[def-separable-space]], and with the second-variable-linear convention of\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. On $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ define the bosonic\ncreation and annihilation maps $a^\\dagger(h)$ and $a(h)$ and the fermionic maps\n$c^\\dagger(h)$ and $c(h)$ as in\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. Write $a_n(h)$ and $a_n^\\dagger(h)$ for\ntheir restrictions to the $n$th bosonic sector. For bosons set\n\n$$D(a(h))=\\{\\Psi\\in\\mathcal F_s(H):\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\|a_n(h)\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\},\\qquad D(a^\\dagger(h))=\\{\\Psi\\in\\mathcal F_s(H):\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\|a_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}.$$\n\nThen $\\mathcal F_s(H)$ and $\\mathcal F_a(H)$ are Hilbert spaces,\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is dense, and it is a graph core for both maximal\nbosonic sector maps. The bosonic operators on their displayed domains are\nclosed and mutually adjoint, and on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ satisfy\n\n$$[a(h),a^\\dagger(k)]=\\langle h,k\\rangle_2I,\\qquad [a(h),a(k)]=[a^\\dagger(h),a^\\dagger(k)]=0.$$\n\nThe fermionic $c(h)$ and $c^\\dagger(h)$ extend to bounded operators on the\nwhole Fock space, with norms $\\|h\\|$, and satisfy on the whole space\n\n$$\\{c(h),c^\\dagger(k)\\}=\\langle h,k\\rangle_2I,\\qquad \\{c(h),c(k)\\}=\\{c^\\dagger(h),c^\\dagger(k)\\}=0.$$\n\nThis is a mathematical construction of the two Fock representations; it does\nnot assign particle statistics to a physical species.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $H$ be a separable\ncomplex Hilbert space with the second-variable-linear convention of\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]], and let $\\mathcal F_s(H)$ and\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ be its bosonic Fock space and finite-particle domain.\nFor $h\\in H$ define on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ the Segal field\n$$\\phi_0(h):=2^{-1/2}\\bigl(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h)\\bigr).$$\nFor $h\\ne0$, this symmetric operator is essentially self-adjoint; write\n$\\Phi(h)=\\overline{\\phi_0(h)}$. If $r=\\|h\\|$, $e=h/r$, $K=e^\\perp$,\n$G=\\mathcal F_s(K)$, choose a countable orthonormal basis $(u_j)$ of $G$\nwhose vectors are finite-particle, and use the one-mode splitting of\n[[lem-qft-fock-one-mode-splitting]] followed by the Hermite unitary\n$|n\\rangle\\mapsto g_n$ into $L^2(\\mathbb R,dx;\\mathbb C)$ with pairing\n$\\langle f,g\\rangle_2=\\int\\overline f g$ to write a vector as\n$(f_j)_{j\\in J}\\in\\ell^2(J;L^2(\\mathbb R,dx;\\mathbb C))$. Then the exact domain and\naction are\n$$D(\\Phi(h))=\\left\\{(f_j):\\sum_{j\\in J}\\int_{\\mathbb R}(1+r^2x^2)|f_j(x)|^2\\,dx<\\infty\\right\\},\\qquad (\\Phi(h)f)_j(x)=rx f_j(x).$$\nFor $h=0$, $\\phi_0(0)$ closes to the zero operator on all of $\\mathcal F_s(H)$.\n\nDefine $\\epsilon(z)=\\bigoplus_{n\\ge0}z^{\\otimes n}/\\sqrt{n!}$ for $z\\in H$.\nThere are unitaries $W(h)$ determined on these total exponential vectors by\n$$W(h)\\epsilon(z)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\|h\\|^2}{4} +\\frac{i}{\\sqrt2}\\langle h,z\\rangle_2\\right) \\epsilon\\!\\left(z+\\frac{i h}{\\sqrt2}\\right).$$\nThey obey\n$$W(h)W(k)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{i}{2}\\operatorname{Im}\\langle h,k\\rangle_2\\right)W(h+k),\\qquad \\langle\\Omega,W(h)\\Omega\\rangle_2=e^{-\\|h\\|^2/4},$$\nand $h\\mapsto W(h)$ is strongly continuous. For every $h$, the one-parameter\ngroup $t\\mapsto W(th)$ has norm derivative at zero exactly on\n$D(\\Phi(h))$, with derivative $i\\Phi(h)$ (for $h=0$ this is the zero\ngenerator). Thus the displayed bounded Weyl operators have precisely the\nclosed Segal fields as their generators; no point-field or measurement claim\nis included.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "claim": "For real $f$, the essentially self-adjoint finite-particle scalar field has closure $\\overline{\\Phi_\\mu(f)}=\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2K_\\mu f)$ by the Segal normalization and [F4]. Thus [F4] identifies this closure as the self-adjoint generator of $t\\mapsto W_\\mu(tf)$. If $O_1\\subseteq O_2$, each real test function supported in $O_1$ is also a real test function supported in $O_2$. Thus the generators and their adjoints for $\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_1)$ belong to the generating set for $\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_2)$; finite sums and products do too, and norm closure preserves inclusion. This proves isotony.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F4",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "claim": "Let $f,g$ be real tests supported in the two spacelike-separated regions. By [F2] their field commutator is zero. Fact [F3], obtained by expanding the fields with the CCR, then gives $2i\\,\\operatorname{Im}\\langle K_\\mu f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2I=0$; hence the imaginary part vanishes. Substituting it in [F1] gives $W_\\mu(f)W_\\mu(g)=W_\\mu(f+g)=W_\\mu(g)W_\\mu(f)$. Taking adjoints shows each generator from the first region commutes with each generator and adjoint from the second. Commutation is preserved under finite linear combinations and products; if bounded sequences $A_n\\to A$ and $B_n\\to B$ in operator norm satisfy $A_nB_n=B_nA_n$, then continuity of multiplication gives $AB=BA$. Taking the two norm closures proves the assertion for the full local algebras.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The statement defines a unital algebra; the empty region has no nonzero tests and step 1.1 still proves isotony."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The zero test is in the generator set, and the algebra includes I by definition."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Isotony and spacelike commutativity of scalar local Weyl algebras: Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ be open. For real $f\\in C_c^\\infty(O)$ define $$W_\\mu(f):=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2K_\\mu f),$$ where the Segal normalization is exactly $\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(h)=(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h))/\\sqrt2$ on the finite-particle domain, as in [[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]]. This scale makes the generator of $t\\mapsto W_\\mu(tf)$ equal to the real scalar field $\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f)$ of [[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]]. Define the unital norm-closed operator algebra $$\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O)= \\overline{\\operatorname{alg}}^{\\|\\cdot\\|} \\{I,W_\\mu(f),W_\\mu(f)^*:f\\in C_c^\\infty(O;\\mathbb R)\\}.$$ Then $O_1\\subseteq O_2$ implies $\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_1)\\subseteq\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_2)$. If every difference $x-y$, with $x\\in O_1$ and $y\\in O_2$, is spacelike, then every element of $\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_1)$ commutes with every element of $\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_2)$. These are algebraic properties of the constructed representation; they are not empirical signaling or measurement claims. The objects have the fixed fiber, test domain, witness or shell specified there. This claim does not vary a finite set cardinality through a singleton boundary; any displayed unit scalar/vector is part of the stated data."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No additional degenerate geometry or quotient case occurs beyond any case explicitly listed above."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Isotony and spacelike commutativity of scalar local Weyl algebras: Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ be open. For real $f\\in C_c^\\infty(O)$ define $$W_\\mu(f):=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2K_\\mu f),$$ where the Segal normalization is exactly $\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(h)=(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h))/\\sqrt2$ on the finite-particle domain, as in [[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]]. This scale makes the generator of $t\\mapsto W_\\mu(tf)$ equal to the real scalar field $\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f)$ of [[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]]. Define the unital norm-closed operator algebra $$\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O)= \\overline{\\operatorname{alg}}^{\\|\\cdot\\|} \\{I,W_\\mu(f),W_\\mu(f)^*:f\\in C_c^\\infty(O;\\mathbb R)\\}.$$ Then $O_1\\subseteq O_2$ implies $\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_1)\\subseteq\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_2)$. If every difference $x-y$, with $x\\in O_1$ and $y\\in O_2$, is spacelike, then every element of $\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_1)$ commutes with every element of $\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_2)$. These are algebraic properties of the constructed representation; they are not empirical signaling or measurement claims. No additional closed-interval endpoint is quantified in this particular assertion. The given strict parameter inequalities and domain-qualified support/series identities are retained; this worksheet does not extend them across a forbidden endpoint."
