        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-scalar-local-weyl-algebra",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]].\nLet $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ be open. For real\n$f\\in C_c^\\infty(O)$ define\n$$W_\\mu(f):=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2K_\\mu f),$$\nwhere the Segal normalization is exactly\n$\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(h)=(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h))/\\sqrt2$ on the finite-particle\ndomain, as in [[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]]. This scale makes the self-adjoint generator\nof $t\\mapsto W_\\mu(tf)$ equal to the closure $\\overline{\\Phi_\\mu(f)}$,\nwhose restriction to $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is the real scalar field\n$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f)$ of\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]]. Define the unital norm-closed operator\nalgebra\n$$\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O)= \\overline{\\operatorname{alg}}^{\\|\\cdot\\|} \\{I,W_\\mu(f),W_\\mu(f)^*:f\\in C_c^\\infty(O;\\mathbb R)\\}.$$\nThen $O_1\\subseteq O_2$ implies\n$\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_1)\\subseteq\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_2)$. If every difference\n$x-y$, with $x\\in O_1$ and $y\\in O_2$, is spacelike, then every element of\n$\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_1)$ commutes with every element of\n$\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_2)$. These are algebraic properties of the constructed\nrepresentation; they are not empirical signaling or measurement claims.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Here\n$\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ is the Schwartz space of smooth complex functions\nwhose coordinate-weighted derivatives are all bounded, as defined in\n[[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]].\nFix an oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski space and an inertial coordinate\nchart as in [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]. Use coordinates\n$x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$ and metric $\\eta=\\mathrm{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, matching the\n$(-+++)$ convention of the SR suppliers. Some QFT sources use its negative\n$(+---)$; the Lorentz group is unchanged, and the phase is fixed explicitly\nbelow.\nCoordinates $x$ have length units, momenta have inverse-length units, and the\nFourier phase below is dimensionless. A parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ has inverse-length\nunits and is only a mathematical shell parameter; it is not identified with a\nphysical rest mass. For $\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3$, set\n$$\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2},\\qquad p_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k).$$\nThe future mass shell is\n$$H_\\mu^+=\\{(p^0,\\mathbf p): (p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2=\\mu^2,\\ p^0>0\\}.$$\nWhen $\\mu=0$, its apex is omitted; thus $\\mathbf k\\ne0$ in the massless\nparameterization. The scalar shell pairing is\n$$p\\circ x:=p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x=-\\eta(p,x).$$\nDefine the measure and one-particle Hilbert space by\n$$d\\nu_\\mu(p_\\mu(\\mathbf k))= \\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)},\\qquad H_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu;\\mathbb C),$$\nwhere the omitted massless apex has measure zero. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb\nR^4)$ define its pointwise shell Fourier restriction by\n$$K_\\mu f(p)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}f(x)e^{ip\\circ x}\\,d^4x.$$\nThe Fourier convention is exactly the displayed integral, with no prefactor.\nIf $\\widehat f(\\xi^0,\\boldsymbol\\xi)=\n\\int f(x)e^{-2\\pi i(x^0\\xi^0+\\mathbf x\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\xi)}\\,d^4x$ denotes\nthe standard transform in\n[[cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space]],\nthen\n$$\\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p):=\\int f(x)e^{i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)}\\,d^4x =\\widehat f\\!\\left(-\\frac{p^0}{2\\pi},\\frac{\\mathbf p}{2\\pi}\\right).$$\nIts inverse is\n$$f(x)=(2\\pi)^{-4}\\int_{\\mathbb R^4} \\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p)e^{-i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)} \\,dp^0\\,d^3\\mathbf p.$$\nThus this convention is a time reflection and rescaling of the cited\nSchwartz automorphism and has the stated reciprocal normalization.\nThe proper orthochronous Lorentz group consists of linear maps preserving\n$\\eta$, orientation and the chosen future cone. A Poincare map is\n$x\\mapsto a+\\Lambda x$ with $a\\in\\mathbb R^4$ and $\\Lambda$ in that group.\n\nThis is a mathematical free scalar one-particle construction. It adopts the\nchosen coordinates, measure and Fourier convention; it asserts no experimental\nobservation, physical mass identification or measurement outcome.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $0<r<R$ and $n\\ge 1$. Then there exists a smooth function $\\rho:\\mathbb R^n\\to [0,1]$ such that $\\rho=1$ on $\\overline B_r(0)$ and $\\operatorname{supp}(\\rho)\\subseteq B_R(0)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to\n$L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then:\n\n1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$;\n2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs to $L^1(\\mu)$;\n3. after assigning the value $0$ on the exceptional parameter sets, the\n   section-integral functions are integrable; and\n4. the three integrals agree:\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all vectors $x,y$ in a real or complex inner-product space,\n\n$$|\\langle x,y\\rangle|\\le\\|x\\|\\,\\|y\\| ,$$\n\nwith equality if and only if $x$ and $y$ are linearly dependent.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "claim": "Choose $\\delta>0$. By [F4], choose a nonnegative nonzero $\\chi\\in C_c^\\infty((-\\delta,\\delta))$. Choose two nonnegative nonzero $u,v\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$ supported in closed balls of radii $r_1,r_2$ whose center separation $D$ satisfies $D-r_1-r_2>2\\delta$. Such bumps follow by translating and rescaling [F4]. Set $f(t,\\mathbf x)=\\chi(t)u(\\mathbf x)$ and $g(t,\\mathbf x)=\\chi(t)v(\\mathbf x)$. For every point in the two supports, $|t-s|<2\\delta$ and $|\\mathbf x-\\mathbf y|\\ge D-r_1-r_2>2\\delta$; therefore all support differences are spacelike. For example, $\\delta=1$, $r_1=r_2=1$ and $D=5$ give a spatial gap of $3>2$, so these strict inequalities are attainable.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "claim": "Insert a radial regulator $e^{-\\epsilon|\\mathbf k|}$, $\\epsilon>0$, in\nthe massless two-point kernel. Angular integration using [F3] gives for\n$r=|\\mathbf x-\\mathbf y|>0$ and $\\tau=t-s$\n$$W_\\epsilon(\\tau,r)= \\frac{1}{4\\pi^2r}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-(\\epsilon+i\\tau)q}\\sin(qr)\\,dq =\\frac{1}{4\\pi^2\\bigl(r^2+(\\epsilon+i\\tau)^2\\bigr)}.$$\nThe integral identity follows by integrating the two exponentials in the\nsine and using $\\operatorname{Re}(\\epsilon+i\\tau)>0$. On the compact\nsupport product in 1.1, $r\\ge d:=D-r_1-r_2>2\\delta>|\\tau|$. Thus the\ndenominator is bounded away from zero uniformly as $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$,\nand $W_\\epsilon$ converges uniformly there to\n$[4\\pi^2(r^2-\\tau^2)]^{-1}$, which is strictly positive.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-3-1",
          "claim": "The regulated shell integrals converge to\n$\\langle K_0 f,K_0 g\\rangle_2$ by dominated convergence [F5]: the absolute\nvalue of the unregulated product is integrable by Cauchy–Schwarz [F7],\nbecause $K_0f,K_0g\\in H_0$. To identify the regulated shell integral with\nthe spacetime pairing of $W_\\epsilon$, Fubini [F6] applies because its\nabsolute integral is at most\n$\\|f\\|_1\\|g\\|_1\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-\\epsilon|\\mathbf k|}\nd^3\\mathbf k/[(2\\pi)^3 2|\\mathbf k|]<\\infty$.\nThe formula in 2.1 and uniform convergence on the compact support product give\n$$\\langle K_0 f,K_0 g\\rangle_2 =\\int f(t,\\mathbf x)g(s,\\mathbf y) \\frac{d^4x\\,d^4y}{4\\pi^2(|\\mathbf x-\\mathbf y|^2-(s-t)^2)}>0,$$\nbecause both test functions are nonnegative and nonzero and the denominator\nis positive on the support product. More explicitly,\n$\\langle K_0f,K_0g\\rangle_2\\ge\n(\\int f)(\\int g)/[4\\pi^2(D+r_1+r_2)^2]>0$, since\n$|\\mathbf x-\\mathbf y|^2-(t-s)^2\\le(D+r_1+r_2)^2$ there. The zero one-point\nvalues follow from [F1]; commutation follows from [F2] and separation in\nstep 1.1.",
          "step": "3.1",
